[LINK: (Gholamreza Soleimani) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gholamreza_Soleimani] (Commander of the  [LINK: (Basij) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basij] )

Beginning on 28 December 2025, demonstrations erupted across multiple cities in  [LINK: (Iran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran]  amid nationwide unrest against the  [LINK: (Islamic Republic government) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Government_of_Iran]  and a deepening  [LINK: (economic crisis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_economic_crisis] . This event has been the largest uprising since the 1979  [LINK: (Islamic Revolution) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_Revolution] . [LINK: ([c]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-58]  The ensuing crackdown, carried out under  [LINK: (Ali Khamenei) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ali_Khamenei] 's and senior officials' order for live fire on protesters, resulted in  [LINK: (massacres that left tens of thousands of protesters dead) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Iran_massacres] , making them the  [LINK: (largest massacres in modern Iranian history) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_massacres_in_Iran] . [LINK: ([56]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2026j-59]  [LINK: ([57]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-CBS_News-2026b-60]  [LINK: ([58]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-www.iranintl.com-2026a-61]

Initially sparked by frustration over record-high  [LINK: (inflation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inflation_in_Iran] , food prices, and currency depreciation, the protests quickly evolved into a broader movement demanding an end to the current regime. [LINK: ([59]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Parent-2025-62]  Beginning with the  [LINK: (bazaari) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bazaari]  (shopkeepers and merchants) in Tehran's  [LINK: (Grand Bazaar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Bazaar,_Tehran]  and later university students, the demonstrations soon spread to other major cities and small settlements. Protesters chanted  [LINK: (anti-government slogans) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_slogans_against_the_Islamic_Republic_of_Iran] , [LINK: ([60]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Manoto-2025-63]  [LINK: ([61]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2025c-64]  [LINK: ([62]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2025d-65]  and attacked symbols of the government and the  [LINK: (Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islamic_Revolutionary_Guard_Corps]  (IGRC). [LINK: ([63]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Chouire-2026-66]  [LINK: ([64]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-67]

Although largely leaderless, the protests escalated on 8 January following a call for unified protests by  [LINK: (Reza Pahlavi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reza_Pahlavi] . [LINK: ([65]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Gritten-2026-68]  On 8 January, 1.5 million protesters came out to the streets of Tehran and by 9 January 5 million protesters came out nationwide. [LINK: ([38]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-auto2-38]  Pahlavi has called for a peaceful transition and a  [LINK: (referendum) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Referendum]  to decide Iran's future political system. [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Reza_Pahlavi-2026-6]  The Iranian government has  [LINK: (cut off Internet access and telephone services) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Internet_blackout_in_Iran]  in an attempt to prevent protesters from organising. [LINK: ([65]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Gritten-2026-68]  It has accused the United States and Israel of fuelling the protests, [LINK: ([63]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Chouire-2026-66]  which analysts suggest may be a tactic to increase security forces' willingness to kill protesters. [LINK: ([25]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-IranUpdateJanuary11-25]

By 9 January, millions had taken to the streets in protests across all 31 provinces. Amid the internet blackout, Iranian security forces escalated their use of live ammunition against demonstrators. [LINK: ([66]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2026h-69]  Hospitals in Tehran and Shiraz were reported to be overwhelmed by injured protesters, many suffering gunshot wounds. [LINK: ([67]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-BBC-2026-70]  Iran's foreign ministry spokesperson confirmed security forces fired on protesters, raising international concern over human rights. [LINK: ([68]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2026f-71]  In addition, thousands were arrested during the violent crackdown. [LINK: ([69]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Christou-2026b-72]  Despite the blackout, on 10 January 2026, The Guardian documented multiple reports of security forces opening fire on demonstrations, with one eyewitness stating they saw "hundreds of bodies" across Tehran. [LINK: ([70]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Christou-2026a-73]

By late January,  [LINK: (Time) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_(magazine)] ,  [LINK: (The Guardian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Guardian]  and  [LINK: (Iran International) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran_International]  reported that between 30,000 and 36,500 protesters were killed during 8–9 January. [LINK: ([44]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Time_30k_local_health_officials-44]  [LINK: ([45]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-guardian30k-45]  [LINK: ([46]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-36,500iranint-46]  As of 29 January, the US-based  [LINK: (Human Rights Activists News Agency) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_Rights_Activists_in_Iran]  (HRANA) had documented a total of 23,570 cases, including 6,479 deaths, of whom at least 6,092 were protesters, and 17,091 additional cases under review. [LINK: ([71]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-hrana-day-33-74]


# BACKGROUND #

Beginning in 2024, Iran's economy experienced sharp inflation, a devalued currency, and an energy deficit, culminating in repeated electricity and gas disruptions and apologies from Iranian president  [LINK: (Masoud Pezeshkian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Masoud_Pezeshkian] . Iran had also suffered from major declines in global influence such as with the  [LINK: (fall of the Assad regime) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fall_of_the_Assad_regime]  in Syria, a major ally. [LINK: ([72]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-AL-Monitor-2024-75]  In the final months of 2025, Iran's economy experienced an unprecedented surge in  [LINK: (exchange rates) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exchange_rate] , a sharp depreciation of the  [LINK: (Iranian rial) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_rial]  with the  [LINK: (US dollar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/US_dollar]  reaching approximately 145,000  [LINK: (Iranian tomans) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_toman] . [LINK: ([73]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-76]  [LINK: ([74]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-77]  Additionally, the country's state statistics centre reported a year-on-year inflation rate of 42.2% in December 2025, an increase of 1.8% compared to November. [LINK: ([51]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-AP_News-2025-53]  Food prices rose by 72%, while health and medical goods increased by 50% year-on-year. [LINK: ([51]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-AP_News-2025-53]

Iran is experiencing a  [LINK: (mismanaged water crisis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water_scarcity_in_Iran] . [LINK: ([52]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Salem-2025-54]  Reports in Iranian media also indicated that the government planned to raise taxes with the start of the  [LINK: (Iranian new year) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nowruz]  on 21 March, fuelling further concern among citizens. [LINK: ([51]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-AP_News-2025-53]  Some protest messaging linked economic hardship to criticism of the government's foreign policy priorities; during the December 2025 demonstrations, some participants chanted " [LINK: (Neither Gaza nor Lebanon, My Life for Iran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neither_Gaza_nor_Lebanon,_My_Life_for_Iran] ". [LINK: ([75]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-BBC_News-2025-78]  Discontent in Iran has also been alleged to have been due to political corruption, with protesters accusing the Iranian government of authoritarianism and prioritising proxies such as  [LINK: (Hezbollah) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hezbollah]  and  [LINK: (Hamas) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamas]  over domestic needs. [LINK: ([53]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Barin-2025-55]  Additionally, Iran faces challenges from ethnic secessionist movements from the  [LINK: (Kurds) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kurdish_separatism_in_Iran] ,  [LINK: (Azerbaijanis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Azerbaijani_separatism_in_Iran] ,  [LINK: (Khuzestani Arabs) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arab_separatism_in_Khuzestan] , and  [LINK: (Balochs) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sistan_and_Baluchestan_insurgency]  and from major powers like the United States and Israel. [LINK: ([76]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-79]

Inflation had surged to 48.6% in October 2025 and 42.2% in December, straining household budgets. [LINK: ([51]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-AP_News-2025-53]  On 29 December, the Iranian rial reached its lowest value (1.45 million to the US dollar), then by 3 January, the government increased the value of the rial to 1.38 million in an attempt to control the people. This had no effect, and on 6 January, the rial broke its record low again (reaching 1.5 million to the US dollar), causing a sharp increase in prices, including food and other essential goods. [LINK: ([77]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Bozorgmehr-2025-80]  [LINK: ([51]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-AP_News-2025-53]  The economic crisis, which had been developing over several years, is accompanied by fears of renewed conflict following the June 2025  [LINK: (Twelve-Day War) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twelve-Day_War]  with Israel and renewed UN  [LINK: (nuclear) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nuclear_program_of_Iran] -related  [LINK: (sanctions) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_sanctions_against_Iran]  imposed through the  [LINK: ("snapback" mechanism) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snapback_mechanism_of_sanctions_against_Iran] . [LINK: ([78]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Radio_Free_Europe/Radio_Liberty-2026-81]  [LINK: ([51]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-AP_News-2025-53]

Economic analysts cited government monetary and fiscal policies, economic mismanagement, chronic budget deficits, and the continuation of  [LINK: (international sanctions) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_sanctions_against_Iran]  as key contributing factors. These conditions directly affected trade guilds, particularly businesses dependent on imports. Severe exchange-rate volatility left many merchants unable to price goods, secure supplies, or continue economic activity. [LINK: ([79]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-BBC_News_Persian-2025a-82]  [LINK: ([80]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-DW_Persian-83]  [LINK: ([81]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-84]  [LINK: ([77]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Bozorgmehr-2025-80]  Economic uncertainty grew in Iran throughout 2025. During the 2025 Twelve-Day War Iran's  [LINK: (nuclear programme) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nuclear_program_of_Iran]  was targeted, and its nuclear facilities were also  [LINK: (struck by the United States) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Midnight_Hammer] . [LINK: ([82]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Faucon-2025-85]  [LINK: ([51]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-AP_News-2025-53]  In September 2025, the  [LINK: (United Nations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations]  reimposed sanctions on Iran through the snapback mechanism, freezing Iranian assets abroad, halting arms transactions, and imposing penalties related to the country's  [LINK: (ballistic missile programme) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran%27s_ballistic-missile_program] . [LINK: ([78]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Radio_Free_Europe/Radio_Liberty-2026-81]  [LINK: ([51]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-AP_News-2025-53]  Many Iranians fear a broader confrontation involving the  [LINK: (United States) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States] , which contributed to market instability. [LINK: ([51]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-AP_News-2025-53]

According to  [LINK: (The Guardian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Guardian] , the economic crisis was the catalyst for the protests; however, they had expanded into an expression of grievances against government corruption. [LINK: ([59]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Parent-2025-62]  It further reported of voices calling for the overthrow of the government, and distrust in the government's calls for dialogue, seeing them as self-serving and deceptive. [LINK: ([59]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Parent-2025-62]   [LINK: (NPR) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NPR]  reported that months before the protests, public anger and frustration had been mounting due to severe energy shortages, civil rights abuses and widespread corruption, and that the protests sparked concerns that they could deteriorate into something much more serious. [LINK: ([83]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-86]

According to  [LINK: (The Atlantic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Atlantic] , the political character of the protests was manifested by protesters chanting " [LINK: (Death to the Dictator) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death_to_the_Dictator] " in reference to  [LINK: (Supreme leader of Iran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supreme_leader_of_Iran]   [LINK: (Ali Khamenei) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ali_Khamenei] , [LINK: ([84]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Azizi-2026-87]  and their loss of faith in Pezeshkian, who was elected in 2024 on the platform and promises of good governance, but had overseen water and electricity cuts, while failing to deliver on the promise of lifting  [LINK: (internet censorship) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet_censorship_in_Iran] . [LINK: ([84]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Azizi-2026-87]  Pezeshkian also promised to meet with protest representatives, and recognised "the constitutional right of peaceful protest", [LINK: ([84]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Azizi-2026-87]  although he lacks control over Iranian security forces. By 1 January 2026, dozens of protesters had been arrested and there were several documented cases of security forces firing live ammunition at protesters from all age groups. [LINK: ([84]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Azizi-2026-87]  Students at  [LINK: (Shahid Beheshti University) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shahid_Beheshti_University]  released a statement declaring that "This criminal system has taken our future hostage for 47 years. It won't be changed with reform or with false promises". [LINK: ([84]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Azizi-2026-87]

