The protests were described as Iran's largest since 2022, when  [LINK: (nationwide demonstrations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mahsa_Amini_protests]  erupted following the death of 22-year-old  [LINK: (Mahsa Amini) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mahsa_Amini]  while in police custody for allegedly wearing her  [LINK: (hijab) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hijab]  improperly. [LINK: ([51]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-AP_News-2025-53]  On 30 December, Ellie Borhan, a British-Iranian activist, viewed this wave of protests as stronger than previous ones. [LINK: ([85]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Weiniger-2025-88]  Iranian public faith in their government faded since the 2022 crackdown on the  [LINK: (Woman, Life, Freedom movement) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woman,_Life,_Freedom_movement]  during the  [LINK: (Mahsa Amini protests) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mahsa_Amini_protests] . [LINK: ([72]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-AL-Monitor-2024-75]  Protests were previously held in  [LINK: (May 2025 by truck drivers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/May_2025_Iranian_protests]  beginning in  [LINK: (Bandar Abbas) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bandar_Abbas] , who blocked roads and ports in Iran due to discontent over low salaries, high insurance rates, and possible hikes in fuel prices in the future. [LINK: ([86]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-89]

Protest slogans have shifted ideologically compared to the 2022 protests. Some new chants increasingly reflect  [LINK: (monarchist) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monarchism_in_Iran]  sentiments. [LINK: ([87]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-90]  Already in June 2025, during the  [LINK: (Iran–Israel war) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran%E2%80%93Israel_war] , the exiled  [LINK: (crown prince) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crown_prince]   [LINK: (Reza Pahlavi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reza_Pahlavi]  had intensified his political efforts and appealed to the  [LINK: (international community) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/International_community]  to help the Iranian people force oust  [LINK: (Ali Khamenei) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ali_Khamenei] 's  [LINK: (theocratic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theocracy]  rule while offering himself up as interim leader to take over running the country. [LINK: ([88]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-POLITICO-2025-91]  In comparison to the Mahsa Amini protests (2022–2023) which were mainly fuelled by girls and women, young men played larger roles in later rounds of the 2025–2026 protests. [LINK: ([89]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-92]

Market traders were influential during the  [LINK: (1979 Islamic Revolution) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_Revolution] , helping to mobilise public support that ultimately led to the overthrow of the monarchy. [LINK: ([51]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-AP_News-2025-53]  The demonstrations were notable in the context of a large-scale government crackdown on dissidents, including arrests of prominent opponents and the  [LINK: (highest number of executions) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capital_punishment_in_Iran]  in nearly 40 years. [LINK: ([82]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Faucon-2025-85]  Executions in Iran have reportedly doubled in 2025 compared to 2024; the execution trends were on the rise since 2022, with activists alleging that the Islamic Republic aims to use executions to instil fear in their population and therefore suppress internal opposition. [LINK: ([90]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-93]

Kurdish-majority regions in Iran have previously undergone severe repression stemming from the Amini protests in 2022, leading to fears of ethnic crackdowns from the government. This was in part due to Iran's accusing Kurdish opposition groups of having incited the 2022 protests. Despite this, Kurdish opposition groups have continued to call for solidarity in the nationwide protests and strikes. Iran has also repeatedly accused Kurdish militias from Iraq of attempting to incite unrest, including in the protests in 2026. [LINK: ([91]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-94]  [LINK: ([65]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Gritten-2026-68]  [LINK: ([92]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Glynn-2026-95]  [LINK: ([93]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-96]  Likewise, Baloch regions in Iran, long suffering from underdevelopment and political exclusion, have also previously been subject to violent crackdowns in the Amini protests. [LINK: ([94]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-97]  On 10 December 2025, Iranian Baloch- [LINK: (Sunni) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunni_Islam]  militant groups like the  [LINK: (Jaysh al-Adl) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jaysh_al-Adl]  announced a merge into a united organisation called the Jebhe-ye Mobaarezin-e Mardomi ( [LINK: (People's Fighters Front) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/People%27s_Fighters_Front] ). In its coalition video, the union rejected  [LINK: (Shia Islam) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shia_Islam] -led clerical rule in the Islamic Republic. The same day, the group carried out an attack on an  [LINK: (Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islamic_Revolutionary_Guard_Corps]  regional command, killing four of its soldiers and wounding three others; it announced responsibility for the attack the next day. [LINK: ([95]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-98]


# PROTESTS #

On 28 December, groups of shopkeepers and merchants at Alaeddin Shopping Centre in Tehran and other commercial centres, including Charsou Mall, went on strike by closing their shops. Simultaneously, protest gatherings formed around these locations, and images and videos of widespread shop closures circulated on social media. [LINK: ([80]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-DW_Persian-83]  According to reports, protesters cited the rising dollar exchange rate and market instability, warning that continued conditions would lead to the bankruptcy of many small and medium-sized businesses. Some gatherings extended into surrounding streets, including  [LINK: (Jomhuri Street) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jomhuri_Eslami_Street] . [LINK: ([79]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-BBC_News_Persian-2025a-82]  [LINK: ([96]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2025a-99]  [LINK: ([97]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-100]  Other people had joined in with the shopkeepers to protest against economic conditions at Jomhuri Street. Elsewhere, iron traders at Iran closed their shops in similar protests of the devalued currency. [LINK: ([98]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-101]

Around the time that the protests began, the value of the Iranian rial sunk to a record low of 1.45 million per US dollar before slightly recovering to 1.38 million. [LINK: ([99]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Shahla-2025-102]  The rial had lost approximately 40 percent of its value since the Iran–Israel war, in part due to the sinking of oil revenue from US sanctions. The year on year inflation rate was up at 42.2 percent. The protests were first started by shopkeepers who sold electronic goods in central Tehran who shut down their stores. [LINK: ([77]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Bozorgmehr-2025-80]  State media revealed blurred footage of initially smaller-scale protests from merchants. [LINK: ([99]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Shahla-2025-102]

Videos and eyewitness accounts showed groups of merchants chanting slogans against economic mismanagement and in some cases expressing anti-government sentiments. [LINK: ([96]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2025a-99]  Protesters also chanted " [LINK: (Law Enforcement) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Police_Command_of_the_Islamic_Republic_of_Iran] , support, support", calling on security forces to back the protests. [LINK: ([100]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-103]  The protesters' main demands included stabilising exchange rates, addressing merchants' economic hardships, creating a predictable business environment, and preventing losses caused by market volatility. [LINK: ([79]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-BBC_News_Persian-2025a-82]  [LINK: ([96]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2025a-99]  There were no reported clashes with security forces on this day and it remained peaceful. [LINK: ([101]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-104]

The protests continued into their second day on 29 December and expanded across various parts of Tehran, including the  [LINK: (Grand Bazaar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grand_Bazaar,_Tehran] . Merchants and shopkeepers closed their businesses and gathered in the streets to protest the unprecedented collapse of the rial and sharp increases in currency and gold prices. Protesters voiced opposition to economic conditions and government management, citing declining purchasing power and rising living costs. Videos shared online showed continued gatherings around Lalehzar, Chaharsouq, and Jomhuri Street, with participants largely non-violent while conveying critical messages toward government economic policies. [LINK: ([102]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-105]  [LINK: ([103]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-106]  [LINK: ([104]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-BBC_News_Persian-2025b-107]  The merchants at the Grand Bazaar in Tehran joined the electronic goods shopkeepers who had started the protests the previous day. [LINK: ([77]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Bozorgmehr-2025-80]

Footage verified by independent sources showed crowds at malls near Tehran's Grand Bazaar chanting "freedom" ( [LINK: (Persian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persian_language] : آزادی,  [LINK: (romanised) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romanization_of_Persian] : Âzâdi). [LINK: ([82]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Faucon-2025-85]   [LINK: (Law enforcement forces) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Police_Command_of_the_Islamic_Republic_of_Iran]  used  [LINK: (tear gas) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tear_gas]  to disperse demonstrators outside the Alaeddin Shopping Centre. [LINK: ([104]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-BBC_News_Persian-2025b-107]  Protests also spread to other cities in Iran. [LINK: ([105]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2025e-108]  On the night of 29 December 2025, protests were reported in several regions across Iran, including Qeshm in the south, and Zanjan and Hamadan in the north. Demonstrators chanted slogans critical of the supreme leader, including " [LINK: (Death to the Dictator) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death_to_the_Dictator] " on  [LINK: (Qeshm Island) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qeshm_Island]  and "Seyyed Ali [Khamenei] will be toppled this year" in  [LINK: (Zanjan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zanjan,_Iran] . [LINK: ([106]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-109]  [LINK: ([82]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Faucon-2025-85]  A video and photo of an unidentified protester went viral, who defiantly sat in the middle of the  [LINK: (Jomhuri Eslami Street) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jomhuri_Eslami_Street]  at Tehran and refused to move for motorbike security forces, but later was beaten and forced to leave. The protester became known as  [LINK: (Tehran's Tank Man) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tehran%27s_Tank_Man] , a reference to the  [LINK: (Tank Man) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tank_Man]  during the  [LINK: (1989 Tiananmen Square protests and massacre) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1989_Tiananmen_Square_protests_and_massacre] . [LINK: ([107]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-110]  [LINK: ([108]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-111]  [LINK: ([85]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Weiniger-2025-88]  [LINK: ([109]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-112]

By the third day of protests, strikes and security measures had expanded, with shops closing in parts of Tehran such as Shoush and Molavi, as well as in Isfahan's  [LINK: (Naqsh-e Jahan Square) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naqsh-e_Jahan_Square] . Heavy security deployments were reported in Tehran, Mashhad, and at  [LINK: (Khajeh Nasir University) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/K._N._Toosi_University_of_Technology] . Government responses included ordering temporary closures in 11 provinces, including Tehran province, due to cold weather and energy constraints. Security forces fired on protesters in Hamadan and deployed tear gas in Tehran and Malard. [LINK: ([105]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2025e-108]  [LINK: ([110]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:8-113]

Demonstrations spread to additional cities, including  [LINK: (Kermanshah) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kermanshah] , Shiraz,  [LINK: (Yazd) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yazd] , and parts of Tehran such as Shadabad and Shush. Students from universities including  [LINK: (Amirkabir) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amirkabir_University_of_Technology] ,  [LINK: (Beheshti) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shahid_Beheshti_University] , Khajeh Nasir,  [LINK: (Sharif) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sharif_University_of_Technology] ,  [LINK: (Science and Culture) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/University_of_Science_and_Culture]  and  [LINK: (Tehran Science and Technology) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran_University_of_Science_and_Technology]  as well as  [LINK: (Isfahan University of Technology) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isfahan_University_of_Technology]  and  [LINK: (Yazd University) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yazd_University]  joined rallies, chanting slogans such as "Death to the Dictator", " [LINK: (Death to Khamenei) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death_to_Khamenei] ", " [LINK: (Neither Gaza nor Lebanon, My Life for Iran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neither_Gaza_nor_Lebanon,_My_Life_for_Iran] ", "We are all together", and "Seyyed Ali (Khamenei) will be toppled this year". [LINK: ([110]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:8-113]  [LINK: ([111]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-114]  [LINK: ([112]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-115]  [LINK: ([113]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Malekian-2025-116]  [LINK: ([114]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-117]  President  [LINK: (Masoud Pezeshkian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Masoud_Pezeshkian] , called on the government to listen to citizens' demands. In response, a government spokesman said a Communication Group would be implemented. [LINK: ([115]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-118]  [LINK: ([116]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-119]  Pezeshkian's comments do not appear to have appeased the protesters, whose demands go beyond just economic stability. [LINK: ([113]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Malekian-2025-116]  Furthermore, some Iranians have expressed scepticism in the government's ability to solve the economic problems, citing previous government statements that they are unable to do much about solving the economic problems. [LINK: ([117]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Al_Jazeera-2025-120]

Human rights organisations and  [LINK: (Gen Z) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generation_Z]  student groups reported that 11 protesters were arrested in the Shoush Square area in Tehran and that five students were detained and four were later released. [LINK: ([118]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2025b-121]  [LINK: ([119]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-122]  [LINK: ([120]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-123]  Another news report published that one student was severely injured at Tehran's Amirkabir University during a crackdown on a campus gathering by members of the  [LINK: (Basij) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basij]  militia of the  [LINK: (Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islamic_Revolutionary_Guard_Corps] . [LINK: ([118]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2025b-121]  Videos on social media appeared to show students chanting slogans critical of the government, removing signs associated with the office of the Supreme Leader's representatives, and confronting security forces at university entrances. [LINK: ([118]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2025b-121]

The people of Isfahan, Kermanshah, and  [LINK: (Fasa) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fasa]  gathered on the fourth day of protests. In Fasa, people held a large rally in front of the governor's office, and in Kermanshah, the markets went on a complete strike. According to reports, police fired live ammunition and tear gas, at the protesters. [LINK: ([121]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-124]  [LINK: ([122]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-125]  At the same time, in  [LINK: (Shirvan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shirvan,_Iran] , working and retired teachers gathered in front of the Education Department. In Kermanshah, repressive forces have been deployed from Ferdowsi Square to the garage (about 8 kilometres), and the heavy presence of security forces is noticeable. [LINK: ([123]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-126]  One person named Mahdi Samavati was reported to have been killed outside the governor's office protest in Fasa. The semi-official  [LINK: (Mehr News Agency) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mehr_News_Agency]  quoted the governor of Fasa as denying this report. [LINK: ([124]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-127]   [LINK: (The Jerusalem Post) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Jerusalem_Post]  stated on 31 December that Samavati (given name transcribed as Mehdi) was alive according to several sources, and that the rumour of Samavati's death was disinformation by the authorities aiming at frightening the protesters. [LINK: ([125]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-JerusPost_rumors_false-128]  Video recordings disseminated online and distributed by the  [LINK: (People's Mojahedin Organisation of Iran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/People%27s_Mojahedin_Organization_of_Iran]  depict severe confrontations between demonstrators and security forces in several cities, including  [LINK: (Tehran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tehran] ,  [LINK: (Isfahan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isfahan] ,  [LINK: (Shiraz) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shiraz] , and  [LINK: (Kermanshah) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kermanshah] .[ [LINK: (citation needed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Citation_needed] ]

Protester Amirhesam Khodayarifard was killed by a handgun shot to the head by an Iranian security force member in a protest in  [LINK: (Kuhdasht) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kuhdasht] ,  [LINK: (Lorestan province) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lorestan_province]  on 31 December. [LINK: ([126]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Hengaw-2026b-129]  The state-run  [LINK: (IRNA news agency) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islamic_Republic_News_Agency]  and Mehr confirmed the death and stated that Khodayarifard was a member of the  [LINK: (Basij) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basij] . [LINK: ([127]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-basij-Kohdasht-130]  Government authorities pressured Khodayarifard's family to  [LINK: (state that he had been a Basij member) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Iran_massacres#False_Basij_membership:_Amirhesam_Khodayarifard]  and called for  [LINK: (online social media) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_networking_service]  silence on the topic. [LINK: ([126]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Hengaw-2026b-129]  The shooting occurred during clashes with protesters. According to Mehr, 13 police officers and Basij members were injured. [LINK: ([127]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-basij-Kohdasht-130]  [LINK: ([128]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Associated_Press-2026-131]

The government ordered nationwide total business shutdown in most of the country due to "cold weather", [LINK: ([129]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-132]  although some analysts say that the real intention is to stifle protests. [LINK: ([130]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-133]  The shutdown was applied to 21 out of Iran's 31 provinces. [LINK: ([131]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-134]  The government began threatening to crack down on protesters, [LINK: ([117]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Al_Jazeera-2025-120]  and the  [LINK: (US State Department) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U.S._State_Department]  stated that they were concerned about protesters "facing intimidation, violence, and arrests". [LINK: ([132]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-135]  Video footage records protesters like merchants, women's rights activists, and students commonly shouting the slogans "Death to the dictator" and "Neither Gaza nor Lebanon, my life for Iran". [LINK: ([53]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Barin-2025-55]  [LINK: ([59]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Parent-2025-62]  In response to the ongoing protests, the Iranian government appointed Abdolnasser Hemmati, a former economics minister, as the new  [LINK: (governor of the Central Bank of Iran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Governor_of_the_Central_Bank_of_Iran] , following Mohammad Reza Farzin's resignation. [LINK: ([133]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-136]

On the fifth day of protests, workers and employees of the central fruit and vegetable market in Tehran stopped working and joined the nationwide uprising by stopping the distribution cycle. Chanting the slogan, "You know with zeal, support support", the protesters called on marketers and the general public to strengthen the national will for change by expanding the strikes. Police officers used tear gas to disperse the demonstrators. [LINK: ([134]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-137]  According to reports, Sarira Karimi, secretary of the faculty council of the Faculty of Law and Political Science and a member of the faculty council of the University of Tehran, who had been arrested on 31 December 2025, was released on 1 January 2026. [LINK: ([135]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-138]

Protesters were reported to have gathered in  [LINK: (Marvdasht) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marvdasht]  and chanted slogans against the Islamic Republic government, such as "This is the year of blood, Seyyed Ali is overthrown". [LINK: ([136]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2026a-139]  In Mashhad, protesters gathered at  [LINK: (Saadi Metro Station) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saadi_Metro_Station] , where riot police attempted to disperse the crowd with force. [LINK: ([136]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2026a-139]  In the  [LINK: (Sistan and Baluchestan province) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sistan_and_Baluchestan_province] , a group of Baluch prisoners released a statement calling on locals to join the wider protests and urged for slogans like "Death to the dictator" and "Baluchestan is awake and despises dictatorship". [LINK: ([137]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-140]

In  [LINK: (Lorestan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lorestan_province] , home to the  [LINK: (Lur minority) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lurs] , protesters were reported lighting fires in the streets while also chanting, "This is the year of blood, Seyyed Ali is overthrown". Additional reports claim officers used live ammunition against protesters. [LINK: ([138]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-141]  In  [LINK: (Lordegan County) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lordegan_County] , gatherings took place in several parts of  [LINK: (Lordegan City) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lordegan] , including around the governor's office and the municipality square. According to these reports, as tensions escalated, some individuals attempted to damage government and bank buildings. Police used tear gas to disperse the crowds, and clashes were reported between the two sides. Several people were injured during the unrest, and unconfirmed reports suggested that multiple deaths had occurred. [LINK: ([139]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-142]  At least three people, including a boy, were killed in Lordegan. [LINK: ([140]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-143]  [LINK: ([141]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Hengaw-2026a-144]  There was a heavy presence of government forces in  [LINK: (Qom) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qom] .

On 2 January, according to media reports, protests continued in large numbers in Tehran, Qom, Isfahan, Shiraz,  [LINK: (Ilam) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ilam,_Iran] , Mashhad, Karaj, Zanjan,  [LINK: (Hamadan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamadan] , and Qeshm. [LINK: ([142]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-145]  In  [LINK: (Zahedan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zahedan]  and Tehran, protests became active again. Funerals for protesters killed by the security forces were held in  [LINK: (Fuladshahr) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fuladshahr] ,  [LINK: (Kuhdasht) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kuhdasht] , and  [LINK: (Marvdasht) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marvdasht] , during which participants expressed opposition to the government, including chants of "Death to Khamenei". At the Kuhdasht funeral for Khodayarifard, Basij and IRGC forces were chased away from the funeral with stones and chants. Khodayarifard's father confirmed that his son was not a Basij member. [LINK: ([143]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2026k-146]  In the  [LINK: (Sadaf district) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Sadaf_district&action=edit&redlink=1]  of  [LINK: (Hamadan province) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamadan_province] , protesters were seen  [LINK: (setting fire) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quran_desecration]  to a  [LINK: (Quran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quran]  and attempted to attack a mosque before being stopped by authorities. [LINK: ([144]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-147]

Protests on 3 January were greater in geographic spread and numbers of protesters than on previous days, and the security presence was also greater. [LINK: ([145]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Human_Rights_Activists_in_Iran_News_Agency-2026a-148]  The  [LINK: (National Council of Resistance of Iran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Council_of_Resistance_of_Iran]  (NCRI) and  [LINK: (Human Rights Activists News Agency) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_Rights_Activists_in_Iran]  (HRANA) named some of the locations of demonstrations, including Kazerun, Malekshahi, Kermanshah, Shiraz, Mashhad, Arkavaz, Isfahan, Tehran, Hafshejan, Karaj, Shahrekord, and Fardis. HRANA reported a cumulative count of 16 fatalities since the beginning of the protests, including one member of government security forces. [LINK: ([146]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-149]  [LINK: ([145]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Human_Rights_Activists_in_Iran_News_Agency-2026a-148]  The themes of the protests, as represented by slogans chanted on 3 January, ranged from economic injustice and governance problems to calls for freedom and justice. HRANA viewed the protest aims as having evolved, with "the boundary between trade-related and everyday demands and political demands ha[ving] become blurred, and [the] ongoing protests hav[ing] taken shape on the basis of accumulated, multilayered grievances." [LINK: ([145]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Human_Rights_Activists_in_Iran_News_Agency-2026a-148]

Following statements by US president  [LINK: (Donald Trump) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump] , where he warns Iran that if they shoot protesters, the United States will come to their rescue, [LINK: ([147]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-150]  Supreme Leader  [LINK: (Ali Khamenei) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ali_Khamenei]  responded on 3 January by saying "We will not yield to the  [LINK: (enemy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_opposition] ", and stating that the "rioters must be put in their place." [LINK: ([148]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-151]  [LINK: ([149]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-152]  On the same day, the US State Department made a statement condemning suppression on protesters' funerals. [LINK: ([150]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-153]   [LINK: (Cloudflare) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cloudflare]  reported a 35% decrease in internet traffic in Iran, with  [LINK: (Iranian internet users reporting frequent outages and slow connections) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet_censorship_in_Iran] . [LINK: ([151]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-154]

There was a heavy presence of security forces in the Grand Bazaar of Tehran. [LINK: ([105]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2025e-108]  Protests and strikes took place in at least 20 major cities and small towns throughout Iran. [LINK: ([152]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Human_Rights_Activists_in_Iran_News_Agency-2026b-155]  Donald Trump said that Iranian authorities would be "hit very hard" should additional protesters be killed. [LINK: ([153]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-156]  In Shiraz, videos showed the police assaulting and beating a man on the ground. When protesters threw projectiles at the police, officers moved toward them on motorcycles. Moments later, a protester poured gasoline on one officer setting him alight. [LINK: ([154]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-157]

On the ninth day, the protests continued throughout Iran. In Tehran's Bagh-e Sepahsalar neighbourhood, voices echoed chants of "Death to Khamenei". Near Tehran University, special forces stood on high alert, while reports of widespread strikes emerged from cities such as Marvdasht, where resistance pulsed through daily life. [LINK: ([155]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-auto10-158]  In Yasuj, security forces confronted the families of detainees gathered outside the governor's office. Reports say that the protests have reached the smaller towns of Saman, Sangsar, and Kushk, as part of dissatisfaction of the Iranian people. [LINK: ([156]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-159]

In addition to the cities previously mentioned, protests were reported in several other locations across the country, including Saman in Chaharmahal and Bakhtiari Province, Sangsar in Semnan Province, Zahedan, Fardis in Karaj, Meshkan in Fars Province, and Noorabad in Mamasani. Demonstrations were further documented in Qazvin, Hamedan, Ilam, Mashhad, Neyshabur, Abadeh, Bushehr, Babol, Bojnourd, Kushk in Isfahan Province, Shazand in Markazi Province, as well as the northern cities of Rasht and Sari. According to reports, protesters in these areas gathered in public spaces, chanting slogans and expressing dissatisfaction with the Khamenei government, reflecting the continued spread of nationwide unrest. [LINK: ([157]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-160]  [LINK: ([158]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-161]  [LINK: ([155]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-auto10-158]

In a joint statement, several major  [LINK: (Kurdish) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kurds]  political groups, including the  [LINK: (Kurdistan Democratic Party of Iran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kurdistan_Democratic_Party_of_Iran]  (KDPI),  [LINK: (Komala) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Komala_Party_of_Iranian_Kurdistan] , the Revolutionary Toilers Association, the Kurdistan Toilers Association, the  [LINK: (Khabat Organisation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Organization_of_Iranian_Kurdistan_Struggle] , the  [LINK: (Kurdistan Freedom Party) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kurdistan_Freedom_Party]  (PAK), and the  [LINK: (Kurdistan Free Life Party) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kurdistan_Free_Life_Party]  (PJAK), expressed support for the protests and called on  [LINK: (Kurds in Iran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kurds_in_Iran]  to carry out strikes and demonstrations. [LINK: ([18]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Hengaw-2026c-18]  [LINK: ([19]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Shafaq_News-2026-19]  [LINK: ([159]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Noonan-2026-162]  The cities of  [LINK: (Abdanan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abdanan]  and  [LINK: (Malekshahi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malekshahi_County] , both in the Kurdish region of Iran, were abandoned by the country's security forces, leaving control of the cities to the protesters. [LINK: ([160]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-163]

A  [LINK: (sit-in) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sit-in]  was conducted by protesters at the Grand Bazaar in Tehran. [LINK: ([161]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-AP_News-2026-164]  Merchants at the market held strikes, in particular with many shops in the corridors of the gold and currency, fabric, and footwear and home appliances markets partially or fully closed. The strikes appeared to be spontaneous, and according to some reports the Bazaar became a "war zone". [LINK: ([162]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Human_Rights_Activists_in_Iran_News_Agency-2026c-165]  [LINK: ([163]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Hakamian-2026-166]  The sit-in was dispersed by security forces using tear gas. [LINK: ([161]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-AP_News-2026-164]  The total number of protest locations over the ten days was estimate by HRANA to be 285 locations in 88 cities [LINK: ([164]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-167]  across 27 provinces, with protests having taken place in 22 universities. The slogans of the protests continued to cover a wide range of economic, social and political grievances. [LINK: ([162]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Human_Rights_Activists_in_Iran_News_Agency-2026c-165]

In  [LINK: (Yazdan Shahr) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yazdan_Shahr] , locals reported that police used excessive force against protesters, initially deploying tear gas and later firing live ammunition at civilians. [LINK: ([163]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Hakamian-2026-166]  The security forces' raids on the  [LINK: (Sina Hospital) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Sina_Hospital_(Tehran)&action=edit&redlink=1]  in Tehran and  [LINK: (on the Imam Khomeini Hospital) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Malekshahi_massacre#Hospital]  in  [LINK: (Ilam) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ilam,_Iran] , aiming to arrest injured protesters, gained national attention. In Ilam, families and medical staff resisted the security forces. Security forces' methods of attack at the Ilam hospital included firing tear gas inside the buildings and hospital grounds. The  [LINK: (Minister of Interior) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ministry_of_Interior_(Iran)]  was ordered to investigate the Ilam raid and provide a report. [LINK: ([162]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Human_Rights_Activists_in_Iran_News_Agency-2026c-165]

On 6 January, a total of 15 forced video confessions by arrested protesters had been broadcast on official media. [LINK: ([162]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Human_Rights_Activists_in_Iran_News_Agency-2026c-165]  On  [LINK: (online social media) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Social_networking_service] , Reza Pahlavi called for chants to take place from homes and in streets at 20:00 (8 pm)  [LINK: (IRST) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran_Standard_Time]  on the evenings of 8 and 9 January. He explained the aim as being to "keep [the] demonstrations disciplined, and as large as possible". He promised to "announce the next calls to action" depending on the response to his call. [LINK: ([165]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-168]

According to HRANA, street gatherings, protests and strikes took place in 37 cities in 24 provinces, bringing the total since the beginning of the protests to 348 sites across 111 cities in 31 provinces. Ten universities joined the protest on 7 January, making a total of 45. The total number of televised forced confessions by arrestees rose to 40. Artists and teachers published statements supporting the protests and criticising the security forces' repression against protest participants. [LINK: ([166]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Human_Rights_Activists_in_Iran_News_Agency-2026d-169]

HRANA interpreted the continuation of the protests despite arrests and violence by the security forces as showing that "a significant portion of [Iranian] society [had come to view] the cost of protest as lower than the cost of silence and inaction". Key themes continued to be economic and governance grievances, seen as "two facets of a single issue". HRANA interpreted the artists' and teachers' statements as showing that "professional and cultural sectors [we]re increasingly aligning themselves with the protest narrative". [LINK: ([166]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Human_Rights_Activists_in_Iran_News_Agency-2026d-169]

Militants of the  [LINK: (Baloch nationalist) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baloch_nationalist]  militant organisation  [LINK: (People's Fighters Front) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/People%27s_Fighters_Front]  (PFF) assassinated Mahmoud Haqiqat, the police chief of  [LINK: (Iranshahr) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranshahr,_Iran] . [LINK: ([21]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-caliber.az-2026-21]  [LINK: ([22]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Deutsche_Welle-22]  [LINK: ([159]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Noonan-2026-162]  IRGC-affiliated media reported that protesters killed two  [LINK: (Law Enforcement Command) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Police_Command_of_the_Islamic_Republic_of_Iran]  officers during protests in Lordegan as well as an unspecified security force member in Malekshahi. [LINK: ([159]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Noonan-2026-162]  In  [LINK: (Mashhad) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mashhad] , protesters were seen lowering a massive  [LINK: (flag of the Islamic Republic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_flag]  and later ripping it in half. [LINK: ([167]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-170]

The  [LINK: (Komala Party of Iranian Kurdistan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Komala_Party_of_Iranian_Kurdistan]  called for a  [LINK: (general strike) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_strike]  in Iran, receiving support from six other Iranian Kurdish opposition parties. The authorities attempted to forcibly disperse the existing protests over economic conditions in the cities of western Iran ( [LINK: (Iranian Kurdistan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_Kurdistan] ) ahead of the announced date, reportedly with the use of live ammunition. [LINK: ([168]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Fr24-0-171]  [LINK: ([169]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-DP7Jan-172]

Protests escalated after 20:00, the time specified in Reza Pahlavi's calls for demonstrations. Immediate telephone line and  [LINK: (internet cuts) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet_censorship_in_Iran]  occurred in several cities, following a common government practice before it commits intense crackdowns. [LINK: ([32]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Euronews-32]   [LINK: (Starlink) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Starlink]   [LINK: (satellite internet) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satellite_internet]  service was unaffected, allowing some users to bypass government-controlled internet blackouts. [LINK: ([170]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-173]  Crowds chanting in Tehran appeared to be mainly pro-Pahlavi.  [LINK: (CBS News) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CBS_News]  described the protests as reaching "a possible  [LINK: (tipping point) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tipping_point_(sociology)] ", [LINK: ([171]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-174]  [LINK: ([172]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-175]  [LINK: ([173]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-176]  and according to  [LINK: (Euronews) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euronews]  it represented "a new escalation in the protest movement". [LINK: ([32]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Euronews-32]  In  [LINK: (Qaemiyeh) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qaemiyeh] , protesters pulled down a statue of  [LINK: (Qasem Soleimani) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qasem_Soleimani] , an IRGC commander who was  [LINK: (assassinated by the United States in 2020) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Assassination_of_Qasem_Soleimani]  and declared a martyr by the Islamic Republic shortly thereafter. [LINK: ([174]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-177]  In Mashhad, a group of protesters had taken down and torn up a large flag of the Islamic Republic. [LINK: ([175]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-178]

Norway-based human rights organisation  [LINK: (Hengaw) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hengaw]  claimed that two  [LINK: (IRGC Ground Forces) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IRGC_Ground_Forces]  members were killed during the protests in  [LINK: (Kermanshah) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kermanshah] . [LINK: ([92]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Glynn-2026-95]  A police officer in  [LINK: (Malard County) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malard_County]  at the Tehran province was killed from a stabbing after attempts to control local unrest. [LINK: ([176]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Agence_France_Presse-2026-179]  Human rights groups have also verified a video showing "distressed family members" in Ghadir hospital in Tehran, looking through a body-pile of protesters killed by Islamic Republic security forces. [LINK: ([70]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Christou-2026a-73]

Protesters took to the streets of Iran on Friday night, videos and eyewitness reports show. [LINK: ([177]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2026c-180]  Pahlavi asked US president Donald Trump to support the Iranian protesters. [LINK: ([178]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-181]   [LINK: (The Economist) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Economist]  reported that the protests had grown to be the biggest  [LINK: (since 2009) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2009_Iranian_presidential_election_protests] , while "some veteran Iran-watchers thought the protests were the biggest since the overthrow of the shah in 1979." [LINK: ([179]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-182]   [LINK: (NDTV 24x7) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NDTV_24x7]  reported a viral protest trend of Iranian women lighting cigarettes to burn pictures of Khamenei in videos, gaining popularity on social media platforms like  [LINK: (X) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twitter] ,  [LINK: (Reddit) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reddit] ,  [LINK: (Instagram) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Instagram] , and  [LINK: (Telegram) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Telegram_(software)] . Because burning Khamenei's image is illegal in Iran, observers have interpreted the videos as deliberate acts of defiance, with the women rejecting state authority over their personal freedoms. [LINK: ([180]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-183]  The trend has been recorded by multiple other news outlets, which similarly note rejection of strict religious and governmental standards over women. [LINK: ([181]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-184]  [LINK: ([182]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-185]  [LINK: ([183]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-186]

Khamenei addressed the protests in a brief televised appearance. [LINK: ([184]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Hindustan_Times-2026-187]  [LINK: ([185]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2026d-188]  In his address, Khamenei called President Trump "arrogant", saying that his hands were stained with the blood of Iranians, and further stating that Trump would be overthrown like other arrogant leaders. [LINK: ([184]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Hindustan_Times-2026-187]  [LINK: ([185]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2026d-188]  He described the protesters as harmful individuals and rioters. [LINK: ([186]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-189]  A fire broke out at an  [LINK: (Islamic Republic of Iran Broadcasting) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islamic_Republic_of_Iran_Broadcasting]  office in  [LINK: (Isfahan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isfahan] . [LINK: ([187]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-190]  Protesters also set fire to buildings in Tehran, including  [LINK: (mosques) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mosque]  in the  [LINK: (Gholhak) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gholhak]  and  [LINK: (Sa'adat Abad) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sa%27adat_Abad]  neighbourhoods. [LINK: ([188]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-191]  Opposition media reported that clashes between protesters and security forces in Kermanshah Province had killed at least 10 IRGC  [LINK: (Kermanshah Nabi Akram Corps) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kermanshah_Nabi_Akram_Corps]  members. [LINK: ([189]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Bern-2026a-192]   [LINK: (Gholam-Hossein Mohseni-Eje'i) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gholam-Hossein_Mohseni-Eje%27i] , Iran's judiciary chief, stated that protesters would face decisive and severe punishment, applied to the fullest extent of the law. [LINK: ([190]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-193]  Senior  [LINK: (American intelligence) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_Intelligence_Community]  officers told  [LINK: (Axios) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axios_(website)]  that their evaluation that these protests were not capable of destabilising the regime was "being reassessed". [LINK: ([191]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-194]

US president  [LINK: (Donald Trump) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump]  warned Iran's authorities against killing demonstrators while praising Iranians as "brave people" amid nationwide protests on Thursday. [LINK: ([177]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2026c-180]  The  [LINK: (Twemoji) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twemoji]  emoji library changed the Iran  [LINK: (flag emoji) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flag_emoji]  from the flag of the Islamic Republic to the  [LINK: (modern design) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lion_and_Sun_flag#Modern_design]  of the  [LINK: (Lion and Sun flag) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lion_and_Sun_flag] . [LINK: ([192]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2026g-195]  Airline flights from the United Arab Emirates, Qatar, Oman, and Turkey to Iranian cities were cancelled amid the mass protests. [LINK: ([193]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Farhat-2026-196]  As of 9 January, protests across all 31 provinces left millions in the streets, with at least 217 killed in Tehran alone, while hospitals in Tehran and Shiraz were overwhelmed by injured protesters, many with gunshot wounds. [LINK: ([194]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Jamalpour-2026-197]  [LINK: ([67]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-BBC-2026-70]  In addition, thousands were arrested by the violent crackdown. [LINK: ([69]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Christou-2026b-72]  Nobel laureate  [LINK: (Shirin Ebadi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shirin_Ebadi]  warned that, under the  [LINK: (Internet blackout) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Internet_blackout_in_Iran] , the Islamic Republic may massacre the protesters. [LINK: ([195]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2026e-198]  Despite the internet outage, on 10 January 2026,  [LINK: (The Guardian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Guardian]  documented multiple reports of Islamic Republic security forces opening fire on demonstrations, causing many casualties among the protesters, with one eyewitness stating they saw "hundreds of bodies" throughout Tehran. [LINK: ([70]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Christou-2026a-73]

Doctors at hospitals in Tehran and Shiraz reported being overwhelmed by large numbers of injured protesters, with some facilities suspending non-urgent admissions and surgeries due to the influx of patients, many of whom suffered gunshot wounds to the head and eyes. [LINK: ([67]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-BBC-2026-70]  In an audio message sent to CNN, an Iranian doctor in the city of  [LINK: (Nishapur) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nishapur]  stated that Iranian security forces killed "at least 30 people" and "among them were children", they further stated that "a 5-year-old child was shot while in their mother's arms." [LINK: ([196]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Sangal-2026-199]  According to the doctor's description, security forces had shot pedestrians and bystanders as well. [LINK: ([196]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Sangal-2026-199]  They added that "Hospitals are extremely chaotic and patients terrified to admit and be identified, for this reason, we are trying to inform people and treat them privately in clinics." [LINK: ([196]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Sangal-2026-199]  The Kurdistan National Guard announced that its Zagros Tornado units attacked an IRGC base in  [LINK: (Nurabad, Lorestan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nurabad,_Lorestan] , and injured three IRGC members. [LINK: ([20]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Sleiman-2026-20]

During the midnight until dawn, Tehran municipality workers were reportedly tasked to clear and collect the  [LINK: (cartridge cases) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cartridge_(firearm)]  off the streets and to deliver them to security forces. [LINK: ([197]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-bbc20260110-200]  Despite the continued internet shutdown imposed by Islamic Republic authorities, thousands of protesters gathered in Tehran and throughout Iran overnight on 9–10 January, chanting "Death to Khamenei", and "Long live the shah". [LINK: ([70]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Christou-2026a-73]  This followed a call by Reza Pahlavi for protesters to seize control over the city centres and hoist the pre-regime  [LINK: (Lion and Sun flag) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lion_and_Sun_flag] , with a promise he would return to Iran soon. [LINK: ([70]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Christou-2026a-73]  The Internet blockade disrupted everyday life, including digital transactions, as well as the functioning of hospitals, pharmacies, banks, and bureaus. Many businesses did not open. [LINK: ([197]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-bbc20260110-200]

The Internet outage has prevented proper documentation of the size of the demonstrations, as well as the extent of police brutality against the protesters; [LINK: ([70]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Christou-2026a-73]  Iranian Nobel peace prize winner Shirin Ebadi had issued a warning on 9 January 2026, about the possibility of a planned "massacre under the cover of a sweeping communications blackout", stating that she had already heard testimonies reporting hundreds of wounded protesters at a single Tehran hospital. [LINK: ([70]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Christou-2026a-73]  On 10 January 2026,  [LINK: (The Guardian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Guardian]  received additional reports via Starlink, stating: "We're standing up for a revolution, but we need help. Snipers have been stationed behind the Tajrish Arg area [one of the affluent areas of Tehran]." [LINK: ([70]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Christou-2026a-73]  Another protester testified that throughout the city, many protesters had been shot, stating, "We saw hundreds of bodies", while a third testimony from a protester confirmed this by saying that they had witnessed a "very high" number of protesters being killed as security forces opened fire on them. [LINK: ([70]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Christou-2026a-73]  Human Rights activists stated that the testimonies were consistent with the reports they had received. [LINK: ([70]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Christou-2026a-73]  The Guardian stated that despite the Internet blackout, protesters had requested that international media cover the reports of increasing police brutality, with one activist saying "please make sure to state clearly that they are killing people with live ammunition." [LINK: ([70]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Christou-2026a-73]

According to The Guardian, much of the international community, including the EU and the US, showed clear support for the protesters. [LINK: ([70]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Christou-2026a-73]  US Secretary of State  [LINK: (Marco Rubio) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marco_Rubio]  posted on  [LINK: (X (formerly Twitter)) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X_(formerly_Twitter)] , "The United States supports the brave people of Iran", [LINK: ([70]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Christou-2026a-73]  and US president  [LINK: (Donald Trump) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump]  "Iran is looking at FREEDOM, perhaps like never before. The USA stands ready to help!!!" [LINK: ([198]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-201]   [LINK: (Mohammad Movahedi-Azad) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mohammad_Movahedi-Azad] ,  [LINK: (attorney general of Iran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosecutor-General_of_Iran] , stated that protesters may be charged as the "enemy of god", or  [LINK: (moharebeh) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moharebeh] , a crime prosecutable by death, according to  [LINK: (CBS News) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CBS_News] , which also reported that state media's reports of order and "no news of any gathering or chaos in Tehran and most provinces" were contradicted by a photo of ongoing demonstrations in  [LINK: (Sa'adat Abad) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sa%27adat_Abad] , Tehran, obtained by the  [LINK: (Associated Press) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Associated_Press] , and a surveillance video from  [LINK: (Fars News Agency) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fars_News_Agency]  in which protesters in  [LINK: (Isfahan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isfahan]  threw  [LINK: (petrol bombs) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Petrol_bombs]  and at least one appeared to be firing a  [LINK: (long gun) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Long_gun] . The  [LINK: (Young Journalists' Club) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Young_Journalists%27_Club&action=edit&redlink=1]  [ [LINK: (fa) -> https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%A8%D8%A7%D8%B4%DA%AF%D8%A7%D9%87_%D8%AE%D8%A8%D8%B1%D9%86%DA%AF%D8%A7%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%86_%D8%AC%D9%88%D8%A7%D9%86] ], associated with state media, reported that protesters killed three members of the volunteer  [LINK: (Basij) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basij]  militia of the IRGC in  [LINK: (Gachsaran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gachsaran] . Reza Pahlavi called for protests to continue through Sunday, while also stating in a social media post that he was "preparing to return to my homeland" and that the goal of the protests should be to seize city centres. [LINK: ([199]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-202]  Iranian state media reported that  [LINK: (Artesh) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islamic_Republic_of_Iran_Army]  First Lieutenant Amin Salimi was shot and killed "while on mission" in  [LINK: (Khavaran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khavaran_District]  neighbourhood, eastern Tehran. US-based human rights activists acknowledged his death on 20 January. [LINK: ([200]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:15-203]

According to an analysis of photos by BBC Persian, the protesters in different cities were engaged in violent conflict with government forces until dawn. [LINK: ([201]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:34-204]  A video from  [LINK: (Punak) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Punak]  neighbourhood of Tehran shows that as the government turned off the street lights, the protesters set off fireworks and created a sea of light using their smartphones in defiance. [LINK: ([201]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:34-204]  [LINK: ([202]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-auto13-205]  Deutsche Welle later fact checked and confirmed that the video was fabricated using artificial intelligence technology and old footage, with the aim of misleading people's understanding of the protests in Iran. [LINK: ([203]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-206]  Videos published by BBC Persian shows explosions amid protests in  [LINK: (Kerman) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kerman]  and gunshots in  [LINK: (Mashhad) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mashhad] . [LINK: ([201]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:34-204]  Unlike previous days, on Saturday mostly IRGC and Basij were mobilised, who use live ammunition, according to witnesses in Tehran and Karaj. [LINK: ([204]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-auto14-207]  On 10 January,  [LINK: (Iran International) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran_International]  reported that at least 2,000 protesters had been killed over the previous 48 hours alone amidst the  [LINK: (internet blackout) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Internet_blackout_in_Iran] , as Iranian security forces escalated their use of lethal force against demonstrators nationwide. [LINK: ([66]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2026h-69]  As a result, all online service have been shut down too, including ATM machines, international phone calls, credit card transactions and business networks, as well as most news sources and social media. [LINK: ([205]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-208]

Khamenei and senior Iranian officials said they were willing to talk to the protesters about economic issues, but also characterised the unrest as incitement by "rioters" and said the protests were funded by foreign powers (the United States and Israel). Iranian state media reported that President Masoud Pezeshkian gave a speech accusing foreign "terrorists" of inciting the protests; Pezeshkian also mentioned: "We are determined, and have decided, to resolve economic problems by any means possible" [LINK: ([206]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-209]  [LINK: ([207]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-210]

Parliament speaker  [LINK: (Mohammad Bagher Ghalibaf) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mohammad_Bagher_Ghalibaf]  gave a speech during a parliament session in which he warned of United States military bases and regional ships and Israel would both be subject to retaliation if Iran is attacked by the United States in reference to Trump's prior threats. In the session, hardliner politicians went at the  [LINK: (dais) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dais]  and shouted " [LINK: (Death to America) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death_to_America] ". Demonstrators rallied in Paris, Vilnius, and London in solidarity with the ongoing protests in Iran and urged Western governments to support the Iranian people seeking freedom. [LINK: ([208]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-211]   [LINK: (The New York Times) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_New_York_Times]  reported that Trump was briefed on military options on Iran but did not yet make a final decision. [LINK: ([209]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-212]

[LINK: (Forbes) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forbes]  reported that the Iranian government successfully shut down the  [LINK: (Starlink) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Starlink]  internet amidst the  [LINK: (internet blackout) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Internet_blackout_in_Iran] . [LINK: ([210]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Doffman-213]  Other sources say Iran has successfully disrupted Starlink network connectivity nationwide, reporting that up to 80% of Starlink traffic was interrupted due to coordinated jamming operations. [LINK: ([211]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-214]  [LINK: ([212]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-215]  [LINK: ([213]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-216]  During a  [LINK: (solidarity rally) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Iranian_diaspora_protests#Violence_towards_diaspora_protesters]  in  [LINK: (Los Angeles) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Angeles]  on 11 January, a  [LINK: (U-Haul) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/U-Haul]  truck was used to  [LINK: (ram into protesters) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vehicle-ramming_attack]  at  [LINK: (Westwood) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Westwood,_Los_Angeles] . [LINK: ([214]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-nbcla-217]  [LINK: ([215]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-cnnla-218]  In Iran, fighters from the Balochi People's Fighters Front killed one Law Enforcement Command officer and injured another in an attack on an LEC patrol vehicle in  [LINK: (Dashtiari County) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dashtiari_County] , Sistan and Baluchistan Province. [LINK: ([25]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-IranUpdateJanuary11-25]

In Tehran, a pro-government rally was held to counter the protests. Videos posted on YouTube showed large crowds of people flying the Iranian flag and chanting Islamic slogans; [LINK: ([31]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Thousands_participate_in_pro-31]  [LINK: ([41]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Christou-2026c-41]  however, reports from Iran International stated the images and videos were  [LINK: (altered) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Video_manipulation] . [LINK: ([216]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-www.iranintl.com-2026b-219]  [LINK: ([217]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-frane24-ai-220]  President  [LINK: (Masoud Pezeshkian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Masoud_Pezeshkian]  was seen taking part in one rally. [LINK: ([218]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-221]

US president Donald Trump stated that Iran has reached out to the United States to negotiate its nuclear programme, following his threat to strike the Islamic Republic over its violent crackdown on protesters. [LINK: ([219]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-222]  Also  [LINK: (Esmail Baghaei) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Esmail_Baghaei] , spokesperson for the  [LINK: (Iranian Ministry of Foreign Affairs) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ministry_of_Foreign_Affairs_(Iran)] , publicly confirmed that security forces had directly fired on protesting citizens, statements that drew widespread attention and concern both domestically and internationally, highlighting criticism of the Iranian government's handling of the demonstrations, raising questions about the proportionality of its response, and drawing scrutiny from human rights organisations regarding the broader state of civil liberties and fundamental rights in the country. [LINK: ([68]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2026f-71]

On 13 January,  [LINK: (Iran International) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran_International]  reported that at least 12,000 people had been killed, describing the massacre as the "largest  [LINK: (killing in Iranian contemporary history) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_massacres_in_Iran] ". [LINK: ([56]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2026j-59]   [LINK: (CBS News) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CBS_News]  reported that 12,000 people have been killed, and possibly 20,000, as Iran's phone services were being restored, and new information was being released. [LINK: ([57]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-CBS_News-2026b-60]

President Trump urged Iranians to keep protesting and stated that help was on the way, with no details. Trump said in a post on  [LINK: (Truth Social) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Truth_Social] : [LINK: ([220]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-223]  [LINK: ([221]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-224]

'"Iranian Patriots, KEEP PROTESTING - TAKE OVER YOUR INSTITUTIONS!!!... HELP IS ON ITS WAY,"'

When a reporter asked what he meant by "help is on its way", he replied, "You're going to have to figure that one out. I'm sorry." [LINK: ([222]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-225]

The Kurdistan Freedom Party assaulted the IRGC's headquarters in Kermanshah and, allegedly, according to the KFP's own claims, successfully infiltrated the headquarters and caused severe IRGC casualties. [LINK: ([223]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-KFParty-226]  Armed Kurdish groups designated as terrorists by Turkey clashed with the IRGC while seeking to cross the border from Iraq and Turkey into Iran; the IRGC had received warning about their movements from Turkey. [LINK: ([224]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-227]

A video, analysed by BBC Verify and BBC Persian, showed, according to forensic examination, nearly 200 bodies scattered in the morgue, many with obvious wounds, including one victim who was only 16 years old. [LINK: ([225]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-228]  The  [LINK: (Iran Human Rights Organisation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran_Human_Rights]  (IHRNGO), based in Norway, said that at least 3,428 protesters were killed by Iranian security forces and at least 10,000 protesters were arrested during the peak of the unrest in Iran from 8 to 12 January. [LINK: ([226]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:16-229]  The head of Iran's judiciary stated that those arrested during the nationwide protests would be swiftly tried and executed. [LINK: ([226]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:16-229]  [LINK: ([227]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:0-230]  [LINK: ([228]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-231]  Washington has threatened military action in response to the crackdown. [LINK: ([227]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:0-230]  [LINK: ([229]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-232]

British and American troops withdrew from  [LINK: (Al Udeid Air Base) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Al_Udeid_Air_Base]  in Qatar. [LINK: ([230]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:1-233]  [LINK: ([231]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:2-234]  The US stated that the withdrawal was a precautionary measure. [LINK: ([231]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:2-234]  "It's a posture change and not an ordered evacuation", a diplomat told Reuters. [LINK: ([230]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:1-233]  Italy and Poland, among other countries, have been urging their citizens to leave Iran "immediately". [LINK: ([232]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-235]  [LINK: ([233]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-236]  [LINK: ([234]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-237]  [LINK: ([235]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-238]  Donald Trump said in the  [LINK: (Oval Office) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oval_Office]  that he had been informed that killings of protesters in Iran had ceased, and he believed that there were "no plans for executions". [LINK: ([236]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-239]  According to AFP, Iranian state television broadcast footage of Donald Trump's  [LINK: (attempted assassination at the 2024 Butler, Pennsylvania rally) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attempted_assassination_of_Donald_Trump_in_Pennsylvania] , accompanied by the Persian message "This time, [the bullet] won't miss", which angered Texas Republican Senator  [LINK: (Ted Cruz) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ted_Cruz]  and other close allies of President Trump. [LINK: ([237]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-240]  [LINK: ([238]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-241]

The Iranian government imposed a nationwide curfew to prevent any protests and deployed security forces and  [LINK: (tanks) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tank]  to patrol towns and cities across the nation. [LINK: ([239]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-isw-jan-15-242]   [LINK: (ISW) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Institute_for_the_Study_of_War]  recorded zero protests on either 14 January or 15 January. [LINK: ([240]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-243]  [LINK: ([239]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-isw-jan-15-242]

The Human Rights Activists News Agency reported that 22,104 protesters had been arrested. [LINK: ([241]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-244]  In Zahedan, a small protest was reported after a  [LINK: (Friday prayer) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Friday_prayer]  sermon by  [LINK: (Sunni) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunni_Islam_in_Iran]  cleric  [LINK: (Abdolhamid Ismaeelzahi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abdolhamid_Ismaeelzahi] , during which he condemned the Iranian government's response to the protests. [LINK: ([242]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-245]  [LINK: ([243]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-246]  In Tehran, the Friday prayer  [LINK: (Imam) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imam] , Ayatollah  [LINK: (Ahmad Khatami) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ahmad_Khatami] , called for the execution of detained protesters. [LINK: ([244]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-247]  [LINK: ([245]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-248]  A slight increase in internet activity to 2% was recorded by  [LINK: (HRANA) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_Rights_Activists_in_Iran] , despite  [LINK: (the restrictions) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Internet_blackout_in_Iran] . [LINK: ([246]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-249]  [LINK: ([247]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-uptick-250]  [LINK: ([248]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-uptick2-251]

ISW recorded zero protests in the streets on 17 January amidst the ongoing enforced curfew. [LINK: ([249]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-252]  On 17 January,  [LINK: (Iran International) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran_International]  reported that security forces were visiting homes and businesses to identify protesters and pressuring them to hand over  [LINK: (CCTV) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CCTV]  footage, while also setting up checkpoints in public areas to detain citizens found with protest images on their phones. [LINK: ([250]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-checkpoints-253]  [LINK: ([251]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-254]

In the evening, a cyberattack was carried out on the Iranian state broadcaster  [LINK: (IRIB) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IRIB] . Hackers hijacked the station's transmissions and aired footage showing exiled Crown Prince Reza Pahlavi, followed by a group of men wearing the uniform of the security forces calling for them not "point your weapons at the people" and instead "join the nation for the freedom of Iran." [LINK: ([252]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-255]

On 19 January,  [LINK: (ABC News) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ABC_News_(United_States)]  reported that the violent crackdown appeared to have quelled the protests. Iranian opposition member  [LINK: (Mehdi Yahyanejad) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mehdi_Yahyanejad] , interviewed by ABC, stated that "the crackdown has been so severe the protests have pretty much come to a halt". [LINK: ([253]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-abc-chant-windows-256]  Due to the ongoing curfew, some Iranians chanted anti-regime slogans from the windows of their homes in  [LINK: (Tehran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tehran] ,  [LINK: (Shiraz) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shiraz] , and  [LINK: (Isfahan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isfahan] . [LINK: ([253]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-abc-chant-windows-256]  [LINK: ([254]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-pbs-chant-windows-257]

On 21 January, Iranian authorities announced that the protests had been suppressed following a severe crackdown. The  [LINK: (Prosecutor-General of Iran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prosecutor-General_of_Iran]   [LINK: (Mohammad Movahedi Azad) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mohammad_Movahedi_Azad]  stated that the unrest had ended. [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Baskar2026-8]

Yousef Pezeshkian, one of president  [LINK: (Masoud Pezeshkian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Masoud_Pezeshkian] 's sons, urged authorities to lift internet restrictions, arguing that blocking access would only delay, not prevent, the spread of images and videos showing the government's violent suppression of protesters. [LINK: ([255]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-258]

U.S. officials said the aircraft carrier  [LINK: (USS Abraham Lincoln) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/USS_Abraham_Lincoln_(CVN-72)]  and its escorting warships entered the Middle East region. [LINK: ([256]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-259]  [LINK: ([257]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-260]  Defence Ministry spokesman  [LINK: (Reza Talaei-Nik) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reza_Talaei-Nik] , said Iran would respond "forcefully" to any US or Israeli attack. [LINK: ([258]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-261]

Six were killed in two explosions that occurred in Iran on January 31, one in  [LINK: (Bandar Abbas) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bandar_Abbas]  and the other in  [LINK: (Ahvaz) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ahvaz] . [LINK: ([259]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-262]


# METHODS #

Nationwide strikes were conducted by businesses, stores, cafes, and workers as well as by online shops and social media influencers. [LINK: ([263]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-266]  [LINK: ([264]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-267]  [LINK: ([265]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-268]  [LINK: ([266]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-269]  [LINK: ([267]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-270]

Protests are in the form of street demonstrations chanting slogans,  [LINK: (car honking) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vehicle_horn] , [LINK: ([268]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-radiozamaneh20260111-271]  [LINK: ([269]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-272]  lighting fires, [LINK: ([268]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-radiozamaneh20260111-271]  and removing surveillance cameras. [LINK: ([270]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-273]  [LINK: ([271]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-274]  In order not to be identified and later arrested, many protesters wear masks and dark clothes, reminiscent of  [LINK: (V for Vendetta) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/V_for_Vendetta_(film)] . [LINK: ([204]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-auto14-207]  As the street lights are turned off by the government, the protesters defiantly set off  [LINK: (fireworks) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fireworks]  and create a sea of light using their smartphones in the darkness of the city. [LINK: ([268]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-radiozamaneh20260111-271]  [LINK: ([201]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:34-204]  [LINK: ([202]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-auto13-205]

During the protests, several notable slogans were chanted by demonstrators, reflecting anti-government sentiments, calls for the restoration of the monarchy, and unity among protesters. These slogans were frequently documented in videos and reports by Persian-language media outlets such as  [LINK: (Manoto) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manoto]  and  [LINK: (Iran International) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran_International] . Many drew on historical references to the  [LINK: (Pahlavi dynasty) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pahlavi_dynasty] , while others directly targeted Supreme Leader  [LINK: (Ali Khamenei) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ali_Khamenei]  or the Islamic Republic's leadership. Symbols associated with the protests included the  [LINK: (Lion and Sun flag) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lion_and_Sun_flag] , which was waved in several demonstrations as a sign of monarchist aspirations and opposition to the government. [LINK: ([261]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2026b-264]  [LINK: ([62]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2025d-65]  [LINK: ([272]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-275]  On 9 January 2026,  [LINK: (X) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twitter]  changed the Iran  [LINK: (flag emoji) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flag_emoji]  from the Islamic Republic flag to the  [LINK: (modern design) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lion_and_Sun_flag#Modern_design]  of the  [LINK: (Lion and Sun flag) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lion_and_Sun_flag] . [LINK: ([192]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2026g-195]

" [LINK: (Death to the Dictator) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death_to_the_Dictator] " ( [LINK: (Persian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persian_language] : مرگ بر دیکتاتور,  [LINK: (romanised) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romanization_of_Persian] : Marg bar Diktâtor)—a general anti-authoritarian chant targeting the government's leadership, reported in Tehran, Isfahan, Kermanshah,  [LINK: (Malard) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malard] , Nahavand,  [LINK: (Noorabad) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Noorabad,_Fasa] , and  [LINK: (Karaj) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Karaj] . [LINK: ([d]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-284]

" [LINK: (Death to Khamenei) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death_to_Khamenei] " ( [LINK: (Persian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persian_language] : مرگ بر خامنه‌ای,  [LINK: (romanised) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romanization_of_Persian] : Marg bar Khâmene'i)—direct call against Supreme Leader Ali Khamenei, heard in Isfahan,  [LINK: (Farsan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Farsan] , Arak, and other protest sites. Previously used during the  [LINK: (2021–2022) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021%E2%80%932022_Iranian_protests]  and  [LINK: (2022–2023 protests) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mahsa_Amini_protests] . [LINK: ([e]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-289]

"Long live the  [LINK: (Shah) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reza_Pahlavi] " ( [LINK: (Persian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persian_language] : جاوید شاه,  [LINK: (romanised) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romanization_of_Persian] : Jâvid Shâh)—repeated chants calling for the return of the  [LINK: (monarchy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monarchism_in_Iran] , heard in  [LINK: (Hamadan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamadan] ,  [LINK: (Arak) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arak,_Iran] ,  [LINK: (Nahavand) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nahavand] ,  [LINK: (Dehloran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dehloran] , and many other cities during nighttime protests. [LINK: ([f]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-296]

" [LINK: (Reza Shah) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reza_Shah] , may your soul be blessed" ( [LINK: (Persian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persian_language] : رضا شاه، روحت شاد,  [LINK: (romanised) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romanization_of_Persian] : Rezâ Shâh, ruhat shâd)—a slogan honouring Reza Shah Pahlavi, founder of the Pahlavi dynasty, chanted in  [LINK: (Hamadan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamadan] ,  [LINK: (Tehran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tehran] ,  [LINK: (Isfahan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isfahan] ,  [LINK: (Kermanshah) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kermanshah] , and other locations. [LINK: ([291]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-297]  [LINK: ([282]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2025g-286]

" [LINK: (Neither Gaza nor Lebanon, My Life for Iran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neither_Gaza_nor_Lebanon,_My_Life_for_Iran] " ( [LINK: (Persian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persian_language] : نه غزه نه لبنان، جانم فدای ایران,  [LINK: (romanised) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romanization_of_Persian] : Na Qazze na Lebnan, jânam fadâ-ye Irân)—a chant that expresses the opposition of some Iranians to the Islamic Republic's military, financial, and political support for  [LINK: (Palestinian militant groups) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palestinian_militant_groups] , neglecting the needs and interests of Iran itself. [LINK: ([75]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-BBC_News-2025-78]  [LINK: ([292]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Weiniger-2026-298]

"Don't be afraid, don't be afraid, we are all together" ( [LINK: (Persian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persian_language] : نترسید، نترسید، ما همه با هم هستیم,  [LINK: (romanised) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romanization_of_Persian] : Natarsid, natarsid, mâ hame bâ ham hastim)—a chant promoting unity and courage among protesters, chanted during nighttime gatherings in Isfahan. [LINK: ([293]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-299]  [LINK: ([279]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2025f-282]

"Dishonourable, dishonourable" ( [LINK: (Persian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persian_language] : بی‌شرف، بی‌شرف,  [LINK: (romanised) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romanization_of_Persian] : Bi-sharaf, bi-sharaf)—directed at security forces or government officials. [LINK: ([294]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-300]

"Freedom, freedom, freedom" ( [LINK: (Persian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persian_language] : آزادی، آزادی، آزادی,  [LINK: (romanised) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romanization_of_Persian] : Āzādi, āzādi, āzādi). [LINK: ([295]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-301]  [LINK: ([284]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-The_New_York_Times-288]

"This is the final battle,  [LINK: (Pahlavi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pahlavi_dynasty]  will return" ( [LINK: (Persian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persian_language] : این آخرین نبرده، پهلوی برمی‌گرده,  [LINK: (romanised) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romanization_of_Persian] : In âkharin nabarde, Pahlavi barmigarde)—a phrase conveying determination for regime change and the return of the Pahlavi family, chanted in Arak,  [LINK: (Rasht) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rasht] ,  [LINK: (Khorramabad) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khorramabad] , Isfahan, Nahavand, and  [LINK: (Dorud) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dorud] . [LINK: ([g]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-304]

"The  [LINK: (Shah) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reza_Pahlavi]  is coming home,  [LINK: (Zahhak) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zahhak]  is overthrown" ( [LINK: (Persian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persian_language] : شاه می‌یاد به خونه، ضحاک سرنگونه,  [LINK: (romanised) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romanization_of_Persian] : Shâh mi-yâd be khune, Zahâk sarnegune)—referencing  [LINK: (Persian mythology) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persian_mythology]  ( [LINK: (Zahhak) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zahhak]  as a tyrant) to symbolise the overthrow of the current government and return of the Shah. [LINK: ([298]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-305]

"Death to the oppressor, whether  [LINK: (Shah) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shah]  or  [LINK: (Rahbar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rahbar] " ( [LINK: (Persian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persian_language] : مرگ بر ستمگر، چه شاه باشد چه رهبر)—a general anti-Islamic Republic and anti-monarchy chant mainly by expressed by supporters of the  [LINK: (National Council of Resistance of Iran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Council_of_Resistance_of_Iran]  (NCRI). [LINK: ([15]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Jones-2026-15]  [LINK: ([16]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Karim-2025-16] [ [LINK: (non-primary source needed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:No_original_research#Primary,_secondary_and_tertiary_sources] ]

As of 7 January, HRANA viewed the protests as being networked. [LINK: ([166]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Human_Rights_Activists_in_Iran_News_Agency-2026d-169]  The  [LINK: (Associated Press) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Associated_Press]  viewed the first steps of protests as "broadly leaderless" before 8 January, and described Reza Pahlavi's influence after the call for demonstration on 8 January as unclear; however, when the time clocked at 8 p.m., chanting broke out across Tehran, with crowds shouting anti-regime slogans and expressing support for the shah's return. [LINK: ([299]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-306]   [LINK: (IranWire) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IranWire]  viewed  [LINK: (Generation Z) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generation_Z]  Iranians as "one of the most visible and active groups" in the 2025–2026 protests, whose political views were strongly impacted by the  [LINK: (Mahsa Amini protests) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mahsa_Amini_protests]  of 2022–2023. [LINK: ([300]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-307]


# GOVERNMENT SUPPRESSION METHODS #

On 8 January 2026, the government imposed significant restrictions on telephone and internet access to limit communication and the dissemination of information. Unlike the  [LINK: (Twelve-Day War) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twelve-Day_War] , there has not been an official internet shut down nationwide. However, connectivity was heavily disrupted in cities experiencing active demonstrations, making it difficult for citizens to send messages, share media, or organise further protests. These measures were widely seen as part of the authorities' efforts to suppress dissent and control the narrative around the unrest. [LINK: ([301]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-308]

The next day, on 9 January, it was reported by multiple media outlets that Iran, in a largely unprecedented measure, had activated military-grade  [LINK: (jammers) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Radio_jamming]  to disrupt civilian  [LINK: (Starlink) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Starlink]  signals. [LINK: ([210]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Doffman-213]  [LINK: ([302]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-iranwire.com-309]  [LINK: ([303]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-310]  Initially, only 30 percent of the media traffic was affected but it rose to 80 percent within several hours. [LINK: ([210]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Doffman-213]  [LINK: ([302]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-iranwire.com-309]  However, from the morning after the blackout began, Islamic Republic authorities issued a "white list" which allowed government affiliated institutions and accounts limited access to the internet, included were government aligned media and  [LINK: (Telegram) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Telegram_(software)]  channels, as well as some universities. [LINK: ([302]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-iranwire.com-309]   [LINK: (Forbes) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forbes]  quoted  [LINK: (VPN) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Virtual_private_network]  expert Simon Migliano [LINK: ([304]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-311]  as saying that "Iran's current nationwide blackout is a blunt instrument intended to crush dissent." [LINK: ([210]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Doffman-213]  Migliano also addressed the cost of the internet shutdown, saying "this 'kill switch' approach comes at a staggering price, draining $1.56 million from Iran's economy every single hour the internet is down." [LINK: ([210]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Doffman-213]  By 11 January, Iran shut down the Starlink internet for the first time. [LINK: ([210]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Doffman-213]

Thereafter, security forces started door-to-door operations, seizing  [LINK: (satellite dishes) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satellite_dish]  amid the blackout to block any external access. [LINK: ([305]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-312]  On 14 January, reports emerged that the government was finalising their internet kill switch project, which was intended to cut the nation completely off from the global internet for extended periods; this plan was being coordinated with  [LINK: (Huawei) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huawei]  and  [LINK: (China) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China] . [LINK: ([306]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:4-313]  By 16 January, after more than 200 hours of the blackout,  [LINK: (NetBlocks) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NetBlocks]  reported a very slight internet connectivity uptick at 2%. [LINK: ([247]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-uptick-250]  [LINK: ([248]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-uptick2-251]  Reports also indicated that the government was planning on permanently cutting internet access, with only vetted individuals being permitted filtered and  [LINK: (censored) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Censorship_in_Iran]  access. [LINK: ([307]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-314]  On 17 January, it was reported that  [LINK: (CCTV) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CCTV]  footage was being confiscated, and checkpoints were set up to detain citizens found with protest images on their phones. [LINK: ([250]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-checkpoints-253]  On 18 January,  [LINK: (Meta) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meta_Platforms]  began hiding the  [LINK: (Instagram) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Instagram]  followers of Iranian residents after reports indicated that Iranian security bodies were extracting large volumes of user data. [LINK: ([308]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-315]  After  [LINK: (targeting Iranians abroad with cybersecurity threats and phishing scams) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Iranian_diaspora_protests#Violence_towards_diaspora_protesters] , it was reported on 19 January that the Iran-linked "Handala Hack" group was routing attacks via Starlink. [LINK: ([309]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-316]  [LINK: ([310]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-317]

On 20 January, it was reported by an investigative watchdog that pro-government editors were active during the crackdown across  [LINK: (Wikipedia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia]  and other services in  [LINK: (deleting content and sanitising articles) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Historical_negationism]  related to the  [LINK: (Islamic Republic's human rights) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_rights_in_the_Islamic_Republic_of_Iran]  record. [LINK: ([311]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-318]  The same day, it was also reported that Iranian authorities claimed they had cut off 40,000 Starlink satellite internet connections during the internet blackout. [LINK: ([312]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-319]  [LINK: ([313]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-320]  On 21 January, it was reported that the Islamic Republic was working on a state-run  [LINK: (intranet) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intranet]  amid the ongoing blackout, similar to that of  [LINK: (North Korea) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet_in_North_Korea] . [LINK: ([314]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-321]  On 23 January, NetBlocks reported the blackout was entering its third week while the Iranian government was attempting to generate false traffic in order to manufacture the narrative of a wider restoration and normalcy. [LINK: ([315]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-322]  During the blackout, Iranian diaspora internet users ran applications to share part of their bandwidth in an attempt to help users inside Iran circumvent the blackout. [LINK: ([316]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-psiphon-323]  Separately, an independent research group stated the Islamic Republic had coordinated a large social media influence operation aimed at shaping global narratives. [LINK: ([317]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-social-media-influence-operation-324]

The presence of  [LINK: (state-sponsored foreign Shia militias) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Involvement_of_foreign_militias_in_the_suppression_of_protests_in_Iran&action=edit&redlink=1]  [ [LINK: (fa) -> https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D9%85%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%AE%D9%84%D9%87_%D9%86%DB%8C%D8%B1%D9%88%D9%87%D8%A7%DB%8C_%D8%B4%D8%A8%D9%87%E2%80%8C%D9%86%D8%B8%D8%A7%D9%85%DB%8C_%D8%AE%D8%A7%D8%B1%D8%AC%DB%8C_%D8%AF%D8%B1_%D8%B3%D8%B1%DA%A9%D9%88%D8%A8_%D8%A7%D8%B9%D8%AA%D8%B1%D8%A7%D8%B6%D8%A7%D8%AA_%D8%AF%D8%B1_%D8%A7%DB%8C%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%86] ], namely the Iraqi  [LINK: (Popular Mobilisation Forces) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Popular_Mobilization_Forces] , Arabic-speaking mercenaries, Lebanese  [LINK: (Hezbollah) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hezbollah] , the Pakistani  [LINK: (Liwa Zainabiyoun) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liwa_Zainabiyoun] , and the Afghan  [LINK: (Liwa Fatemiyoun) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liwa_Fatemiyoun] , in suppressing protests was reported. [LINK: ([318]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-325]  [LINK: ([319]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-326]  [LINK: ([320]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-327]  [LINK: ([321]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-328]   [LINK: (Iran International) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran_International]  reported that on 2 January 2026, Iraqi militias affiliated with the Iranian government recruited forces to assist Iranian security forces in suppressing protests in Iran. [LINK: ([320]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-327]  On 6 January 2026, it was reported that approximately 800 members of Iraqi Shia militia groups, including  [LINK: (Kata'ib Hezbollah) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kata%27ib_Hezbollah] ,  [LINK: (Harakat al-Nujaba) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harakat_Hezbollah_al-Nujaba] ,  [LINK: (Sayyid al-Shuhada) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kata%27ib_Sayyid_ul-Shuhada] , and the  [LINK: (Badr Organisation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Badr_Organization]  had been sent to Iran. [LINK: ([320]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-327]  The troops were reportedly transported through the border crossings of  [LINK: (Shalamcheh) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shalamcheh] ,  [LINK: (Chazabeh) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chazabeh] , and  [LINK: (Khosravi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khosravi,_Kermanshah] , officially under the cover of a "pilgrimage to the holy sites of  [LINK: (Imam Reza) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imam_Reza]  in  [LINK: (Mashhad) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mashhad] ", while in practice they were gathered at a base in  [LINK: (Ahvaz) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ahvaz]  before being dispatched to various regions to assist in suppressing protests. [LINK: ([320]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-327]

Roni Insaz, a former member of the Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps, in his interview with The Jerusalem Post, explained “It’s very difficult for Iranian forces to kill Iranians, because they are the same people,” and added that loyal militias from Syria, Lebanon, Afghanistan, and Pakistan have participated for firing at the protestors. [LINK: ([322]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-l558-329]  According to Iran International, "The reason behind this move by the Islamic Republic could be its concern that the Iranian police might not follow orders to attack unarmed, ordinary people, or simply because its forces are insufficient to stop protests in more than 100 cities". [LINK: ([323]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-330]

On 9 January 2026 the United States warned Iran against using foreign militias to crush protests. [LINK: ([324]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-331]  According to  [LINK: (The Media Line) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Media_Line] , Iraqi Shiite militia members were recruited to help suppress Iranian protesters, receiving $600 each. By 11 January, more than 60 buses, each carrying about 50 people, had crossed the Iraq‑Iran border. [LINK: ([325]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-332]  On 14 January, a source told  [LINK: (IHRNGO) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran_Human_Rights]  that the security forces in the Kurdish regions of Iran during the killings did not speak  [LINK: (Persian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persian_language] , while in Karaj, an eyewitness said the forces spoke  [LINK: (Arabic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arabic_language]  and took  [LINK: (selfies) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Selfie]  with the bodies. [LINK: ([326]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:7-333]  On 15 January, an Iraqi source stated to CNN that "nearly 5,000" fighters from Iraqi militias had crossed into Iran over the preceding weeks. [LINK: ([40]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-5000-iraq-40]

The Iranian government has been accused of using footage of protesters' bodies in morgues to demoralise future protests. [LINK: ([327]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-ABC_News-2026-334]  Families trying to receive the bodies of their loved ones have oftentimes been forced to pay compensation for the bullets that killed their relatives. [LINK: ([327]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-ABC_News-2026-334]  [LINK: ([328]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-www.iranintl.com-335]  Reports stated that security forces and Revolutionary Guard members raided and intimidated the families of protesters who were killed, imposed restrictions on the retrieval and burial of bodies, and warned that families would be charged fees. [LINK: ([328]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-www.iranintl.com-335]  There have been reports that families were unable to locate the remains of their relatives after authorities secretly buried them in locations far from where the deaths occurred. [LINK: ([327]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-ABC_News-2026-334]  [LINK: ([329]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-iran-intl-burials-336]  Reports have also indicated that the authorities retained the remains until families consented to official accounts describing the deceased as aligned with the government and  [LINK: (Basij) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basij]  rather than as protesters. [LINK: ([327]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-ABC_News-2026-334]  [LINK: ([330]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-337]  Likewise, images and videos from the pro-government rallies were reported to have been  [LINK: (altered) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Video_manipulation] . [LINK: ([216]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-www.iranintl.com-2026b-219]  [LINK: ([217]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-frane24-ai-220]  [LINK: ([306]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:4-313]   [LINK: (HRANA) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_Rights_Activists_in_Iran]  also reported cases of  [LINK: (forced confessions) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forced_confessions]  being broadcast. [LINK: ([331]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-hrana-jan18-338]  Furthermore, doctors and healthcare workers were reported to have been arrested or disappeared for providing medical care to protesters or documenting their injuries. [LINK: ([332]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-doctors-disappeared-339]

Sources close to Iran's Supreme National Security Council and the presidential office report that the killing of protesters was carried out on the direct order of Supreme Leader Ali Khamenei, with full approval from senior state officials. The council allegedly authorised live fire, which was executed mainly by the IRGC in what is described as a deliberate, organised operation exceptional in scale and intensity. [LINK: ([58]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-www.iranintl.com-2026a-61]

On 13 January,  [LINK: (The Guardian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Guardian]  reported that Islamic Republic security forces were documented using shotguns and rifles with live ammunition, [LINK: ([333]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:5-340]  as well as heavy  [LINK: (DShK) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DShK]  machine guns against protesters, [LINK: ([326]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:7-333]  with a Tehran doctor stating that security forces were "shooting to kill". [LINK: ([333]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:5-340]  Further reports also indicated the security forces used snipers and knives to attack protesters. [LINK: ([334]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-341]  [LINK: ([335]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-342]  [LINK: ([336]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-343]  [LINK: ([337]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-344]  A spokesperson from the Abdorrahman Boroumand Centre for Human Rights cited evidence that even when using "less lethal" weapons, security forces were deliberately shooting at the heads, eyes, genitals and vital organs of the protesters, so as to terrorise protesters by mutilating them and causing them permanent disability, [LINK: ([333]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:5-340]  reusing  [LINK: (the tactic employed in the 2022 Mahsa Amini protests) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eye_injuries_during_2022_Iranian_protests] . [LINK: ([333]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:5-340]  At least one young girl had been shot in the  [LINK: (pelvic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pelvis]  area and was in critical condition. [LINK: ([333]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:5-340]  A medic in Tehran reported that there were "direct shots to the heads of the young people, to their hearts as well." [LINK: ([338]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-345]

Additionally, multiple testimonies have revealed Iranian security forces raiding hospitals to arrest, [LINK: ([339]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-346]  and in many cases execute, hospitalised protesters. [LINK: ([340]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:6-347]  [LINK: ([326]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:7-333]  On 4 January, according to Namdar Baghaei Yazdi, vice president of the Iranian Medical Society UK, security forces in full riot gear stormed Imam Khomeini Hospital in Ilam, attacked medical personnel with tear gas and shotgun pellets, and arrested injured protesters, [LINK: ([341]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:9-348]  with another similar assault being carried out by security forces on 6 January in Sina Hospital in Tehran. [LINK: ([341]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:9-348]  Yazdi was quoted as saying "Hospitals are no longer sacred in Iran, and we are very concerned for our medical colleagues there who are already at risk from the regime." [LINK: ([341]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:9-348]  A doctor from southern Iran reported that security forces had "finished off" protesters who had been hospitalised at the time, [LINK: ([326]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:7-333]  [LINK: ([340]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:6-347]  further stating "they killed many, arrested many, and many are on the run. The situation is very bad." [LINK: ([340]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:6-347]  According to The Times, another doctor from Tehran stated that security forces had "gone into hospitals and forcibly taken the corpses of protesters with them", and some of the wounded protesters treat their injuries at home and avoid being admitted to the hospital out of fear of being arrested. [LINK: ([342]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-349]   [LINK: (Iran Human Rights) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran_Human_Rights]  reported that in  [LINK: (Rasht) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rasht] , security forces  [LINK: (surrounded and trapped protesters inside the Rasht Bazaar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Rasht_massacre] , set it on fire, and killed people attempting to surrender or escape, while also "finishing off" wounded survivors in the streets and in hospitals. [LINK: ([326]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:7-333]

On 17 January, reports indicated the Iranian government may have used  [LINK: (toxic chemical substances) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chemical_weapon]  against protesters. [LINK: ([343]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-chemical1-350]  [LINK: ([344]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-351]  [LINK: ([345]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-chemical2-352]  Footage showed security forces atop vehicles wearing hazmat suits and masks designed for hazardous chemical materials. [LINK: ([343]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-chemical1-350]  Some victims reportedly died several days after exposure rather than immediately. [LINK: ([343]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-chemical1-350]  [LINK: ([345]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-chemical2-352]  On 18 January, it was reported that the detained protesters were being injected with unknown substances while in custody. [LINK: ([346]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-chemical-injections-353]  On 23 January, chemical gas was reported to have been used on the crowds of protesters and escape routes, causing severe breathing problems, burning pain of the eyes, skin and lungs, vomiting blood, and sudden weakness and loss of movement. [LINK: ([347]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-354]

On 27 January,  [LINK: (The Guardian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Guardian]  reported that the Islamic Republic was concealing protest deaths through  [LINK: (mass graves) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mass_graves_in_Iran]  and burials. [LINK: ([348]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-guardian-mass-burials-355]  [LINK: ([349]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-iran-intl-mass-burials-356]  [LINK: ([329]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-iran-intl-burials-336]

On 5 January 2026, Gholamhossein Mohseni Ejei, head of the judiciary in the Islamic Republic, stated that there would be no leniency for "rioters" despite the right to demonstrate, [LINK: ([350]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:10-357]  [LINK: ([351]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:11-358]  and the judiciary's Mizan news agency quoted him saying "I instruct the attorney general and prosecutors across the country to act in accordance with the law and with resolve against the rioters and those who support them (...) and to show no leniency or indulgence", [LINK: ([350]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:10-357]  [LINK: ([351]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:11-358]  and stressing that the penalty would be "decisive" and "maximum". [LINK: ([352]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-auto1-359]  [LINK: ([352]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-auto1-359]  Regarding the rapid trials and executions or protesters, Iran state television shared a video in which Mohseni-Ejei said "If we want to do a job, we should do it now. If we want to do something, we have to do it quickly, if it becomes late, two months, three months later, it doesn't have the same effect. If we want to do something, we have to do that fast." [LINK: ([353]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-auto-360]  [LINK: ([354]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-361]  On 10 January, Supreme Leader Ali Khamenei said that the demands of protesters in the country are "completely fair", but "rioters" should "be put in their place." [LINK: ([351]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:11-358]  On 13 January, in a televised statement from the office of the Tehran prosecutor, the office declared that an undeclared number of protesters would be charged with " [LINK: (moharebeh) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moharebeh] ", or "waging war against God", an offence  [LINK: (punished by death in Iran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capital_punishment_in_Iran] , and used extensively in the past by the regime's judiciary. [LINK: ([355]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-362]  According to the Human Rights Activists News Agency, as of 14 January 2026, over 18,400 people had been arrested. [LINK: ([353]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-auto-360]  By 18 January,  [LINK: (HRANA) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_Rights_Activists_in_Iran]  reported that 24,669 protesters had been detained. [LINK: ([331]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-hrana-jan18-338]

According to Iran International, on 10 January 2026 the "One Word" lawyers' network, citing the internet shutdown isolating protesters from the outside world, called on the international community and Iranian judges to prevent the show trials and extrajudicial executions of protesters following the orders of Ali Khamenei and senior judicial officials. [LINK: ([356]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:12-363]  In its statement, the network detailed new orders from Khamenei instructing security forces "to deal harshly with protesters in recent gatherings" as well as separate statements from the Head of the Judiciary, the Attorney General of the country, and the Tehran Prosecutor calling for "extraordinary, out-of-order proceedings and the imposition of the most severe punishments in the cases of detained protesters." [LINK: ([356]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:12-363]

On 18 January,  [LINK: (The Guardian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Guardian]  reported that a human rights group claimed detainees were subjected to torture and sexual assault while in custody. [LINK: ([357]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-sexual-assault-364]   [LINK: (Iran International) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran_International]  also reported detainees were being injected with unknown chemical substances. [LINK: ([346]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-chemical-injections-353]  Reports from KHRN (a French-based human rights organisation) cited by Iran International, claimed that two protesters were abused while in detention during Iran's nationwide unrest in the city of  [LINK: (Kermanshah) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kermanshah] . [LINK: ([358]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:14-365]  One of those detained was a minor. According to these accounts, security personnel subjected the detainees to sexualised physical mistreatment during their transfer, including beatings and the use of  [LINK: (batons) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baton_(law_enforcement)]  through their clothing and over their anal areas. [LINK: ([358]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:14-365]


# CASUALTIES #

Reporting on casualties from the 2026 protests has varied widely, from several thousand to around 30,000 deaths, [LINK: ([44]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Time_30k_local_health_officials-44]  [LINK: ([359]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-iran-intl-30k-25-jan-366]  with figures difficult to verify due to media restrictions and the prolonged internet shutdown. Iran's Supreme Leader Ali Khamenei said that "several thousand" people had been killed, while attributing the violence to protesters. Independent and foreign media, citing medical professionals, human rights groups, and leaked hospital data, have reported substantially higher figures. On 17 January,  [LINK: (The Sunday Times) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Sunday_Times]  reported that a network of Iranian doctors estimated between 16,500 and 18,000 deaths and hundreds of thousands of injuries; one doctor quoted in the report described the killings as  [LINK: (genocide) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genocide] . [LINK: ([48]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-SundayTimes17Jan-49]  On 21 January, the Iranian government issued a death toll of 3,117 people, of which 2,427 were "civilians and security forces", according to  [LINK: (Associated Press) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Associated_Press] , which described the Iranian government as having a track record of undercounting deaths during protests. [LINK: ([360]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-367]

On 31 December 2025, during a protest in  [LINK: (Fuladshahr) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fuladshahr] , Dariush Ansari Bakhtiariwand was shot with a  [LINK: (Kalashnikov rifle) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kalashnikov_rifle]  by security forces. He died before reaching medical care. [LINK: ([361]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-368]  While participating in a protest in  [LINK: (Kuhdasht) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kuhdasht]  on 31 December, Amirhesam Khodayarifard (reported to be 21, [LINK: ([127]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-basij-Kohdasht-130]  or 22 years old) [LINK: ([126]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Hengaw-2026b-129]   [LINK: (was shot dead) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Killing_of_Amirhesam_Khodayarifard]  with a bullet to the head by a plainclothes retired IRGC agent. [LINK: ([362]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Aida_Ghajar-2026-369]  Eyewitness testimony and video evidence showed that Khodayarifard was among the protesters. [LINK: ([126]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Hengaw-2026b-129]  Government media stated that protesters had been throwing rocks at security forces, and that Khodayarafid was killed after the rocks had been thrown. [LINK: ([363]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-370]

Governmental media, including  [LINK: (Mehr News Agency) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mehr_News_Agency] , [LINK: ([127]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-basij-Kohdasht-130]  claimed that Khodayarifard was a member of the  [LINK: (Basij) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basij] . The governor of Kuhdasht, an  [LINK: (Imam of Friday Prayer) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imam_of_Friday_Prayer] , and  [LINK: (Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islamic_Revolutionary_Guard_Corps]  (IRGC) members visited Khodayarifard's family. Permission for the family to access Khodayarifard's body and carry out a burial was conditioned on the family making a televised statement stating that Khodayarifard had been a member of the Basij. [LINK: ([126]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Hengaw-2026b-129]  Governmental authorities gave warnings on  [LINK: (Telegram) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Telegram_(software)]  and  [LINK: (Instagram) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Instagram]  forbidding the publication of information about Khodayarifard's killing. [LINK: ([126]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Hengaw-2026b-129]  As of 5 January 2026 [LINK: ([update]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests&action=edit] , the authorities, who were pressuring Khodayarifard's family to state that he was a Basij member, had not given the body to Khodayarifard's family, according to  [LINK: (IranWire) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IranWire] . [LINK: ([362]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Aida_Ghajar-2026-369]

Two protesters, Ahmad Jalil, 21, and Sajjad Valamanesh, 28, were killed in  [LINK: (Lordegan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lordegan]  on 1 January 2026. Both had been shot by security forces and died later from their injuries. [LINK: ([364]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-371]  [LINK: ([365]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-372]  On the evening of 1 January, two men and a teenage boy, Shayan Asadollahi, 30, Vahab Musavi, and Mostafa, 15, residents of  [LINK: (Azna) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Azna,_Lorestan]  (in Lorestan province) were killed by gunfire from security forces. [LINK: ([141]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Hengaw-2026a-144]  The IRGC-aligned  [LINK: (Fars News Agency) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fars_News_Agency]  stated that the protesters had either tried to attack a police station [LINK: ([366]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-373]  or had tried to disarm the security forces. [LINK: ([141]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Hengaw-2026a-144]  Ahmadreza Amani, 28, was shot in the chest by security forces at around 18:00  [LINK: (IRST) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran_Standard_Time]  in Azna and died in hospital. [LINK: ([367]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-374]

Khodadad Shirvani, 33, a  [LINK: (Marvdasht) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marvdasht]  resident, was shot with shotgun pellets by security forces on the same evening in Marvdasht. He died after being transferred to a hospital. [LINK: ([368]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-375]  In  [LINK: (Nurabad) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nurabad,_Lorestan]  (Lorestan province), Ahad Ebrahimpour Abdoli, 35, was lethally shot the same evening with three bullets (one to his heart) by security forces during a protest in Ba'ath Square in Nurabad. Security forces and the Imam of Friday Prayer pressured Abdoli's family to say that he was a Basij member and that he had been shot by "enemy forces". [LINK: ([369]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-376]

On 2 January, a 42-year-old protester, Ali Azizi Jafarabadi, a Kurdish man from  [LINK: (Harsin County) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harsin_County]  was shot dead by security forces in  [LINK: (Harsin) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harsin] . [LINK: ([370]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-377]

On 3 January, the total number of arrested protesters had increased to 132 according to  [LINK: (Hengaw) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hengaw]  [LINK: ([371]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-378]  or 582 according to  [LINK: (HRANA) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_Rights_Activists_in_Iran] . [LINK: ([145]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Human_Rights_Activists_in_Iran_News_Agency-2026a-148]   [LINK: (Iran International) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran_International]  estimated the death count of protesters to be at least eight, the number of locations to be 113 locations in 46 cities across 22 provinces, with at least 44 people shot and wounded by live ammunition or pellet guns fired by Iranian security forces. [LINK: ([105]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2025e-108]  [LINK: ([372]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-379]   [LINK: (Four protesters were shot) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Malekshahi_massacre]  dead with "military-grade" weapons by IRGC members at protests in  [LINK: (Malekshahi County) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malekshahi_County]  in  [LINK: (Ilam province) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ilam_province] ; forty were injured and many taken to hospital. [LINK: ([373]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-380]

By the early morning of 4 January, Iran International reported the death toll from the protests to have risen to at least 16. [LINK: ([374]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-381]  HRANA estimated that since the beginning of the protests there had been 990 arrests and 51 cases of injuries to protesters, mostly from pellet and plastic bullets. [LINK: ([152]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Human_Rights_Activists_in_Iran_News_Agency-2026b-155]

The total number of arrested protesters rose to 1,200 on 5 January. [LINK: ([375]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-382]  Iranian authorities claimed to have arrested a  [LINK: (Mossad) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mossad]  agent partaking in the protests, with the agent allegedly confessed to being recruited, trained by, and continuing communication with Mossad, and said that Mossad handlers told him to go to people's residences, but was later instructed to move his "operations" to local marketplaces. [LINK: ([376]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-383]

On 6 January, a total of 2,076 protesters had been arrested, and at least 34 protesters and 2 police officers had been killed, according to HRANA. [LINK: ([162]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Human_Rights_Activists_in_Iran_News_Agency-2026c-165]

HRANA estimated 140 new arrests of protesters or identifications of previously arrested protesters, making a total of 2,217, including 165 minors and 46 university students. HRANA counted at total since the beginning of the protests as 38 deaths, including 29 adult protesters, 5 minor protesters, and 4 security officers. [LINK: ([166]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Human_Rights_Activists_in_Iran_News_Agency-2026d-169]

The  [LINK: (2026 Fardis massacre) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Fardis_massacre]  was committed by the  [LINK: (Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islamic_Revolutionary_Guard_Corps]  on 8 January 2026 in  [LINK: (Fardis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fardis] ,  [LINK: (Alborz province) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alborz_province] ,  [LINK: (Iran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran] . In response to protests, the Iranian government  [LINK: (suspended internet and telephone) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Internet_blackout_in_Iran]  services nationwide, a tactic  [LINK: (previously employed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2019_Internet_blackout_in_Iran]  when using lethal force against protesters to suppress news and evade scrutiny. According to reports, a massacre occurred in  [LINK: (Fardis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fardis] , where troops reportedly killed 50 protesters with a machine gun. [LINK: ([377]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-2026_Fardis_massacre_GulfNews_45_killed-384]

On 9 January, HRANA estimated that a total of 2,311 protesters had been arrested and at least 65 were dead. [LINK: ([1]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-HRANA-2026-1]   [LINK: (Time) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_(magazine)]  reported that they were in contact with a Tehran-based doctor who informed them that over 217 protester deaths had been recorded across six hospitals in the city, while Iranian Nobel Peace Prize laureate  [LINK: (Shirin Ebadi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shirin_Ebadi]  warned that authorities might carry out a massacre under the cover of the widespread  [LINK: (internet blackout) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Internet_blackout_in_Iran] . [LINK: ([194]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Jamalpour-2026-197]  [LINK: ([195]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2026e-198]  Reports from two hospitals in  [LINK: (Rasht) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rasht]  and Tehran indicate overall 110 dead bodies being transferred to these two hospitals during 8 and 9 January. Some wounded people had military-grade bullets in head and neck, indicating that the shootings were intended to kill. [LINK: ([378]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-385]

Amidst the  [LINK: (internet blackout) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Internet_blackout_in_Iran]  during the protests,  [LINK: (Iran International) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran_International]  stated their most conservative estimates indicated that at least 2,000 people had been killed by government forces over the past 48 hours alone. [LINK: ([66]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2026h-69]  On 10 January, HRANA estimated that 2,638 protesters had been arrested and confirmed that 116 fatalities had occurred. [LINK: ([379]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-386]  The  [LINK: (Centre for Human Rights in Iran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Center_for_Human_Rights_in_Iran]  warned that a "massacre is unfolding." It said hundreds of protesters had been killed since the government cut off internet access, and security forces,  [LINK: (as in the past, shot people in the eyes) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eye_injuries_during_2022_Iranian_protests]  with metal pellets and rubber bullets. It reported that hospitals were overwhelmed, and that casualties continue to rise. [LINK: ([380]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-387]

Iranian state media reported that  [LINK: (Artesh) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islamic_Republic_of_Iran_Army]  First Lieutenant Amin Salimi was shot and killed "while on mission" in  [LINK: (Khavaran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khavaran_District]  neighbourhood, eastern Tehran. US-based human rights activists acknowledged his death on 20 January. [LINK: ([200]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:15-203]

According to a US-based rights group, more than 500 people have been killed in Iran's protests, with 579 additional deaths under investigation (raising the total to 1,123), while over 10,681 people have been arrested. [LINK: ([381]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-388]  The  [LINK: (People's Mojahedin Organisation of Iran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/People%27s_Mojahedin_Organization_of_Iran]  reported that more than 3,000 people had been killed in the protests by 11 January. Their figures, based on local sources, hospitals, and families, show the regime even displayed some bodies on state TV, falsely blaming protesters for their deaths. [LINK: ([382]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-389]  [LINK: ([383]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-390]  By 11 January,  [LINK: (Time) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_(magazine)]  reported that, starting with reports from a handful of Tehran hospitals, an informal, expatriate group of academics and professionals calculated that protester deaths could have reached 6,000 through Saturday the 10th. [LINK: ([384]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Vick-2026-391]

On 12 January,  [LINK: (CNN) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CNN]  reported that given the  [LINK: (government's internet shutdown) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Internet_blackout_in_Iran]  and the slow trickle of information emerging from Iran, the full scale of casualties remains unclear. [LINK: ([385]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-392]

On 13 January,  [LINK: (Iran International) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran_International]  reported that at least 12,000 people had been killed, describing the massacre as the "largest  [LINK: (killing in Iranian contemporary history) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_massacres_in_Iran] ". [LINK: ([56]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2026j-59]   [LINK: (CBS News) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CBS_News]  reported that activist groups in Iran estimated 12,000 people to have been killed, and possibly 20,000, based on medical reports. [LINK: ([57]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-CBS_News-2026b-60]

By 15 January, fatality reports saw a significant increase as minimal internet connectivity was restored. [LINK: ([386]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:3-393]  While confirmed figures from HRANA stood at approximately 2,000-2,500 deaths, [LINK: ([387]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-394]  [LINK: ([386]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:3-393]  other rights organisations such as  [LINK: (Iran Human Rights) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran_Human_Rights]  (IHR) reported at least 3,428 fatalities. [LINK: ([326]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:7-333]  At the same time, leaked internal documents from the  [LINK: (Supreme National Security Council) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supreme_National_Security_Council]  and the presidential office, reported by  [LINK: (Iran International) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran_International] , suggested that as many as 12,000 people may have been killed during the peak crackdown between 8 and 10 January. [LINK: ([388]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-395]   [LINK: (CBS News) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CBS_News]  cited sources within Iran claiming the total death toll could potentially reach 20,000. [LINK: ([389]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-396]  [LINK: ([390]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-397]

On 17 January,  [LINK: (The Sunday Times) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Sunday_Times]  reported that "at least 16,500-18,000" people had been killed, and 330,000 had been injured. [LINK: ([48]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-SundayTimes17Jan-49]  [LINK: ([391]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-330000injured-398]

On 18 January,  [LINK: (HRANA) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_Rights_Activists_in_Iran]  stated that 24,669 protesters had been detained, at least 25 children under 18 years of age had been killed during the protests, and 145  [LINK: (forced confessions) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forced_confessions]  had been broadcast. [LINK: ([331]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-hrana-jan18-338]  The U.S.-based  [LINK: (Human Rights Activists News Agency) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_Rights_Activists_in_Iran]  (HRANA) added that 3,919 people were verified to have been killed during the protests, and a further 8,949 additional deaths were still under investigation. [LINK: ([331]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-hrana-jan18-338]   [LINK: (The Guardian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Guardian]  reported claims that detainees were being subjected to torture and sexual assault while in custody; [LINK: ([357]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-sexual-assault-364]   [LINK: (Iran International) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran_International]  reported detainees were being injected with unknown chemical substances. [LINK: ([346]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-chemical-injections-353]

On 20 January, the Canadian-based International Centre for Human Rights reported that at least 43,000 had been killed by Islamic Republic agents, over 350,000 injured, and over 10,000 blinded in a  [LINK: (similar tactic from the 2022 protests) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eye_injuries_during_2022_Iranian_protests] . [LINK: ([49]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-ichr-20jan-50]  According to the report, more than 95% of the victims were killed on 8 and 9 January amid the internet blackout. [LINK: ([49]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-ichr-20jan-50]

On 22 January,  [LINK: (Iranian state media) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_state_media]  released its first official casualty figures related to the protests, reporting that 3,117 people had been killed. The announcement stated that the dead included both civilians and members of the security forces, but provided no detailed breakdown or methodology for how the figures were compiled. [LINK: ([392]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-399]  [LINK: ([393]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-400]

Independent human rights organisations challenged the official casualty figures.  [LINK: (HRANA) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_Rights_Activists_in_Iran]  reported that the death toll had surpassed 4,900, based on information compiled from hospital records, eyewitness testimony, and regional monitoring networks, and warned that the true number of fatalities was likely higher due to severe reporting constraints. [LINK: ([394]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-401]  HRANA later updated its data to state that the verified total had reached at least 4,519 confirmed deaths with an additional 9,049 cases under review.

On the same day, the  [LINK: (United Nations Special Rapporteur on Human Rights in Iran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations_Special_Rapporteur_on_Human_Rights_in_Iran]  stated the number of civilians killed may exceeded 20,000, citing information received from civil society sources and the cumulative scale of the crackdown. [LINK: ([395]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-un-sr-402]  Separately, the U.S. television network  [LINK: (NewsNation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NewsNation]  reported substantially higher estimates, suggesting the death toll could be closer to 30,000. [LINK: ([396]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-newsnation-jan-22-403]

HRANA, alongside other journalists and independent analysts emphasised that discrepancies between official and independent casualty  figures were driven in part by near-total internet shutdowns, restricted information flows, limits foreign media access, and security pressure on local reporters. These conditions, including arrests and intimidation of journalists, significantly hindered the ability of independent monitors to document fatalities comprehensively and to verify casualty data during the protests. [LINK: ([397]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-404]  [LINK: ([398]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-405]

The HRANA total death count estimate, including both verified cases and deaths under investigation, grew above 20,000 on 24 January, with counts of 5,149 adult protesters, 60 minors, 208 security force members, 42 non-participants, and 17,031 deaths under investigation, making 22,490 in total. HRANA stated that the overall level of repression was unprecedented, with a cumulative number of 205 broadcast false confessions and the total number of arrests reaching 40,887. [LINK: ([399]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-HRANA_28thday-406]

On 25 January,  [LINK: (Time) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_(magazine)]  reported the 8 and 9 January protest-related death toll as 30,304 from hospital records, according to Amir Parasta, a doctor, excluding military hospital records, bodies in morgues, and places not included in the analysis. Time also stated that two senior health officials said that the death toll "could have been" 30,000, and that Time could not independently verify the two health officials' estimate. [LINK: ([44]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Time_30k_local_health_officials-44]  [LINK: ([400]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-407]

The same day,  [LINK: (Iran International) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran_International]  reported more than 36,500 were killed by security forces on 8 and 9 January, based on documents reviewed by Iran International's editorial board, as well as field reports and accounts from medical staff and victims' families. [LINK: ([359]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-iran-intl-30k-25-jan-366]

[LINK: (The Guardian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Guardian]  reported that the Islamic Republic was concealing protest deaths through  [LINK: (mass graves) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mass_graves_in_Iran]  and burials. [LINK: ([348]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-guardian-mass-burials-355]  [LINK: ([349]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-iran-intl-mass-burials-356]  [LINK: ([329]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-iran-intl-burials-336]

According to an  [LINK: (HRANA) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_Rights_Activists_in_Iran]  report on 16 January, Iran carried out at least 52 executions during the protests between 5 and 14 January. [LINK: ([401]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-52-executions-408]  [LINK: ([402]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-hrana-52-409]  On 21 January, HRANA further added that at least 313 prisoners were executed during the time of the protests. [LINK: ([403]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-313-executions-410]

On 12 January 2026, it was reported that 26-year-old protester,  [LINK: (Erfan Soltani) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death_sentence_of_Erfan_Soltani] , was sentenced to be executed on 14 January, making him one of the first of these protesters to be handed an  [LINK: (execution sentence) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capital_punishment_in_Iran] . [LINK: ([404]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Isaacson-2026-411]  Soltani was arrested during protests in  [LINK: (Fardis) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fardis]  on 8 January. [LINK: ([404]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Isaacson-2026-411]  [LINK: ([405]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-412]  Human rights groups and activists reported that Soltani was denied access to a lawyer, a  [LINK: (fair trial) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Right_to_a_fair_trial] , or any opportunity to appeal. [LINK: ([404]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Isaacson-2026-411]  Several days later, Soltani was notified that he was to face execution on 14 January, less that a week after his arrest. [LINK: ([406]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:13-413]  Shortly before the execution date, Iranian Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi denied any plans to hang people, and Soltani's family was notified that his execution had been postponed, while the judiciary stated that the charges against him only consisted of "colluding against national security" and "propaganda activities against the establishment" which are not punishable by death in Iran. [LINK: ([406]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:13-413]  The state broadcasting company IRIB claimed that reports of Soltani's pending execution were a "blatant act of news fabrication." [LINK: ([406]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:13-413]

On 16 January, US president Donald Trump thanked the government for promising to stop 800 planned executions; [LINK: ([407]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-trump-thank-1-414]  [LINK: ([408]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-trump-thank-2-415]  however, Ali Salehi, State Prosecutor in Tehran was seen on state television saying: "Trump says a lot of nonsense and gibberish", and "Our reaction will be forceful, preventive and swift. Indictments have been issued for numerous cases and sent to courts." [LINK: ([409]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-416]  Iran's judiciary chief, Gholamhossein Mohseni Ejei, stated that carrying out the sentences quickly would serve as a deterrent. [LINK: ([410]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-417]  On 19 January 2026, UN rights chief  [LINK: (Volker Türk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volker_T%C3%BCrk]  claimed in a statement that Iran was using executions "as a tool of state intimidation". During 2025, 1,500 people  [LINK: (had been executed in Iran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capital_punishment_in_Iran] . The number of executions in 2025 was second only to  [LINK: (China) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Capital_punishment_in_China] . [LINK: ([411]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-418]

On 17 January, another protester, 19-year-old Amirhossein Ghaderzadeh, was sentenced to death by hanging, with his execution scheduled for 21 January. [LINK: ([412]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-ghaderzadeh-419]

On 20 January, reports circulated that the Iran Human Rights Society (IHRS) said that an Iranian conscript named  [LINK: (Javid Khales) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Javid_Khales&action=edit&redlink=1]  had been sentenced to death after refusing an order to fire on protesters and that he was being held in  [LINK: (Isfahan Prison) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isfahan_Central_Prison] , though this has not been independently verified. [LINK: ([413]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-420]  [LINK: ([414]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-421]

On 22 January 33-year-old Ali Rahbar was reportedly executed in  [LINK: (Mashhad) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mashhad] , becoming one of the first protesters to be executed. [LINK: ([415]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-422]  [LINK: ([416]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-423]  Iran's judiciary denied his execution having taken place, stating that such a person had not even been detained. [LINK: ([417]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-424]

According to activists, human rights advocates, and foreign intelligence, the Islamic Republic secretly executed jailed protesters while disguising the deaths as protest casualties and suicides. [LINK: ([418]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-secret-executions-1-425]  [LINK: ([419]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-secret-executions-2-426]  [LINK: ([420]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-secret-executions-3-427]  [LINK: ([421]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-secret-executions-4-428]  [LINK: ([422]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-429]

Government authorities repeatedly presented fatalities during the protests as members of the security forces killed by protesters, after which evidence from witnesses and family statements showed that the victim had been shot by the security forces. [LINK: ([423]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Hengaw-2025-430]  Government media claimed that Amirhesam Khodayarifard, killed on 31 December, was a member of the  [LINK: (Basij) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basij] . [LINK: ([128]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Associated_Press-2026-131]  Eyewitness reports and video evidence, collected by  [LINK: (Hengaw) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hengaw] , contradicted this claim, in particular showing that he was standing among the protesters and was killed by a shot to the head by a security forces member. [LINK: ([126]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Hengaw-2026b-129]  Initially, authorities conditioned family access to Khodayarifard's body on the family making a televised statement that he was a Basij member. [LINK: ([126]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Hengaw-2026b-129]  During the funeral, which took place on 2 January, Khodayarifard's father confirmed that his son was not a Basij member. [LINK: ([143]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Iran_International-2026k-146]

On 3 January,  [LINK: (Agence France-Presse) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agence_France-Presse]  referred to a statement by  [LINK: (Mehr) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mehr_News_Agency]  that IRGC member Latif Karimi was killed during clashes in  [LINK: (Malekshahi County) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malekshahi_County] , [LINK: ([424]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-431]  during which four protesters were killed by the IRGC. [LINK: ([425]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-432]  On 4 January, eyewitnesses and other sources clarified that Karimi was present among the protesters when he was shot by IRGC members, and died in Imam Khomeini Hospital in  [LINK: (Ilam) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ilam,_Iran] . Karimi was a retired  [LINK: (brigadier-general) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brigadier-general]  by profession. Karimi's son stated on  [LINK: (Telegram) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Telegram_(software)] , "My father's only 'crime' was telling [the government security forces] not to shoot at the people." [LINK: ([423]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Hengaw-2025-430]

On 7 January, militants of the  [LINK: (Baloch nationalist) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baloch_nationalist]  militant organisation  [LINK: (People's Fighters Front) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jaysh_al-Adl#People's_Fighters_Front]  (PFF) assassinated Mahmoud Haqiqat, the police chief of  [LINK: (Iranshahr) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranshahr,_Iran] . [LINK: ([21]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-caliber.az-2026-21]  [LINK: ([22]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Deutsche_Welle-22]  [LINK: ([159]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Noonan-2026-162]  IRGC-affiliated media reported that protesters killed two  [LINK: (Law Enforcement Command) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Police_Command_of_the_Islamic_Republic_of_Iran]  officers during protests in Lordegan as well as an unspecified security force member in Malekshahi. [LINK: ([159]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Noonan-2026-162]

On 8 January, Norway-based human rights organisation  [LINK: (Hengaw) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hengaw]  claimed that two  [LINK: (IRGC Ground Forces) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IRGC_Ground_Forces]  members were killed during the protests in  [LINK: (Kermanshah) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kermanshah] . [LINK: ([92]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Glynn-2026-95]  A police officer in  [LINK: (Malard County) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malard_County]  at the Tehran province was killed from a stabbing after attempts to control local unrest. [LINK: ([176]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Agence_France_Presse-2026-179]

On 9 January, opposition media reported that clashes between protesters and security forces in Kermanshah Province killed at least 10 IRGC Ground Forces  [LINK: (Nabi Akram Unit) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kermanshah_Nabi_Akram_Corps]  members. [LINK: ([189]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Bern-2026a-192]

On 11 January, fighters from the PFF killed one Law Enforcement Command officer and injured another in an attack on an LEC patrol vehicle in  [LINK: (Dashtiari County) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dashtiari_County] , Sistan and Baluchistan Province. [LINK: ([25]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-IranUpdateJanuary11-25]

[LINK: (Shahram Maghsoudi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Shahram_Maghsoudi&action=edit&redlink=1] , powerlifting champion [LINK: ([426]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-433]

[LINK: (Mojtaba Tarshiz) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mojtaba_Tarshiz] , former professional footballer [LINK: ([427]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-434]

[LINK: (Salar Behdari) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Salar_Behdari&action=edit&redlink=1] , goalkeeper in the  [LINK: (Persian Gulf Pro League) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persian_Gulf_Pro_League]  for  [LINK: (Aluminium Arak F.C.) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aluminium_Arak_F.C.]  [LINK: ([428]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-435]

[LINK: (Mohammad Hajipour) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Mohammad_Hajipour&action=edit&redlink=1] , former goalkeeper for the  [LINK: (Iran national beach soccer team) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran_national_beach_soccer_team]  [LINK: ([429]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-sport-thousands-killed-436]

[LINK: (Ahmad Ramezanzadeh) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Ahmad_Ramezanzadeh&action=edit&redlink=1] ,  [LINK: (catcher) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catcher]  for the  [LINK: (Iran national baseball team) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran_national_baseball_team]  [LINK: ([429]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-sport-thousands-killed-436]

[LINK: (Sara Behboudi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Sara_Behboudi&action=edit&redlink=1] , mountaineer [LINK: ([429]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-sport-thousands-killed-436]

[LINK: (Sahba Rashtian) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Sahba_Rashtian&action=edit&redlink=1] , women's football assistant referee [LINK: ([429]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-sport-thousands-killed-436]

[LINK: (Hassan Ghasemi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Hassan_Ghasemi&action=edit&redlink=1] , karate champion and referee [LINK: ([429]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-sport-thousands-killed-436]

[LINK: (Arshia Ahmadpour) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Arshia_Ahmadpour&action=edit&redlink=1] , boxer [LINK: ([429]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-sport-thousands-killed-436]

Canadian foreign minister  [LINK: (Anita Anand) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anita_Anand]  confirmed that a Canadian citizen was killed by the Islamic Republic forces during the protests. [LINK: ([430]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-437]  Several  [LINK: (Afghan migrants) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Afghan_refugees]  were also among those killed in the protests. [LINK: ([431]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-438]


# REACTIONS #

Reactions to the protests ranged from calls for dialogue and economic relief to warnings of force.

Supreme Leader Ali Khamenei said officials should talk to protesters, but added that "rioters must be put in their place". [LINK: ([432]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-439]

As the unrest continued, President Masoud Pezeshkian announced economic measures including changes to foreign-exchange subsidies intended to shift support directly to consumers. [LINK: ([433]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-440]

Vice President  [LINK: (Mohammad Reza Aref) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mohammad_Reza_Aref]  attempted to resign during the first days of the protests, but it was not accepted by President Pezeshkian. [LINK: ([434]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-441]

[LINK: (Mohammad Baqer Qalibaf) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mohammad_Baqer_Qalibaf] , Speaker of the  [LINK: (Islamic Consultative Assembly) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islamic_Consultative_Assembly]  , said "Malicious individuals and organised movements want to turn any kind of public demand and protest into chaos and chaos using their trained agents in the square, but the Iranian nation has repeatedly demonstrated its vigilance, awareness, and compassion for the country's security". [LINK: ([435]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-442]

On 10 January 2026, the IRGC warned that safeguarding security is a "red line". [LINK: ([436]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-443]

Chief Justice  [LINK: (Gholam-Hossein Mohseni-Eje'i) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gholam-Hossein_Mohseni-Eje%27i] , the head of Iran's judiciary, said that the punishment for rioters would "be decisive, maximum and without any legal leniency". [LINK: ([437]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-444]

Attorney general  [LINK: (Mohammad Movahedi Azad) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mohammad_Movahedi_Azad]  warned that anyone taking part in demonstrations is an " [LINK: (enemy of God) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hirabah#Iran] ", a crime that carries the death penalty under Iranian law. [LINK: ([438]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-445]

On 19 January,  [LINK: (Ahmad-Reza Radan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ahmad-Reza_Radan] , Iran's national police chief stated that those who were "deceived" into taking part in the demonstrations, or "riots" as the government defines them, have three days to turn themselves in. [LINK: ([439]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-446]  He also said: Important arrests were made of the main elements of the unrest, who will be punished after going through legal procedures. [LINK: ([440]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-447]

On 20 January the  [LINK: (Iranian Parliament) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Islamic_Consultative_Assembly]  threatened that an attack on  [LINK: (Supreme Leader) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supreme_Leader_of_Iran] , Ayatollah Ali Khamenei will lead to  [LINK: (Jihad) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jihad] . [LINK: ([441]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-448]

The  [LINK: (Iranian diaspora) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_diaspora]  held solidarity rallies worldwide, with some facing  [LINK: (violence and threats during the demonstrations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Iranian_diaspora_protests#Violence_towards_diaspora_protesters] . [LINK: ([442]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-449]  [LINK: ([214]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-nbcla-217]  [LINK: ([443]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-450]

[LINK: (European Union) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Union] : The  [LINK: (European External Action Service) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_External_Action_Service]  urged Iran's security forces to exercise restraint and called on authorities to uphold rights including freedom of expression and peaceful assembly. [LINK: ([444]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-451]  The EU also co-signed the aforementioned joint statement issued by Australia and Canada. [LINK: ([445]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-The_New_Region-452] 
European Commission President  [LINK: (Ursula von der Leyen) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ursula_von_der_Leyen] , urged the release of imprisoned Iranian protesters, condemned the violent crackdown, and called for internet access to be restored, saying Europe stands "fully behind" those demonstrating. [LINK: ([446]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-453]

European Commission President  [LINK: (Ursula von der Leyen) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ursula_von_der_Leyen] , urged the release of imprisoned Iranian protesters, condemned the violent crackdown, and called for internet access to be restored, saying Europe stands "fully behind" those demonstrating. [LINK: ([446]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-453]

The   [LINK: (United Nations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations]  criticised Iran's internet shutdown and violation of civil liberties. On 20 January it was stated that The  [LINK: (UN Human Rights Council) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations_Human_Rights_Council]  would hold an emergency session on Iran on 23 January about the extreme violence in Iran. [LINK: ([447]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-454]  [LINK: ([448]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-455] 
UN Special Rapporteur  [LINK: (Mai Sato) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mai_Sato]  called for an independent investigation to determine whether the Iranian government's suppression of nationwide protests constitutes  [LINK: (crimes against humanity) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crimes_against_humanity] . [LINK: ([449]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-456] 
On 23 January 2026, the UN Human Rights Council (UNHRC) adopted a resolution condemning the violent suppression of protests in Iran. Twenty-five states voted in favor of the resolution, including  [LINK: (Spain) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spain] ,  [LINK: (Italy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italy] ,  [LINK: (Slovenia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slovenia] ,  [LINK: (Mexico) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexico] ,  [LINK: (Colombia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colombia] ,  [LINK: (South Korea) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Korea] , and  [LINK: (Ghana) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghana] . Seven states voted against, including  [LINK: (China) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China] ,  [LINK: (India) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/India] ,  [LINK: (Vietnam) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vietnam] ,  [LINK: (Indonesia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indonesia] , and  [LINK: (Pakistan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pakistan] . Fourteen states abstained, including   [LINK: (Brazil) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brazil] ,  [LINK: (Qatar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qatar] ,  [LINK: (Egypt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Egypt] ,  [LINK: (South Africa) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Africa] ,  [LINK: (Kenya) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kenya] , and  [LINK: (Thailand) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thailand] . [LINK: ([450]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-457]

UN Special Rapporteur  [LINK: (Mai Sato) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mai_Sato]  called for an independent investigation to determine whether the Iranian government's suppression of nationwide protests constitutes  [LINK: (crimes against humanity) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crimes_against_humanity] . [LINK: ([449]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-456]

On 23 January 2026, the UN Human Rights Council (UNHRC) adopted a resolution condemning the violent suppression of protests in Iran. Twenty-five states voted in favor of the resolution, including  [LINK: (Spain) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spain] ,  [LINK: (Italy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italy] ,  [LINK: (Slovenia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slovenia] ,  [LINK: (Mexico) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexico] ,  [LINK: (Colombia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colombia] ,  [LINK: (South Korea) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Korea] , and  [LINK: (Ghana) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghana] . Seven states voted against, including  [LINK: (China) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China] ,  [LINK: (India) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/India] ,  [LINK: (Vietnam) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vietnam] ,  [LINK: (Indonesia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indonesia] , and  [LINK: (Pakistan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pakistan] . Fourteen states abstained, including   [LINK: (Brazil) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brazil] ,  [LINK: (Qatar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qatar] ,  [LINK: (Egypt) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Egypt] ,  [LINK: (South Africa) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Africa] ,  [LINK: (Kenya) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kenya] , and  [LINK: (Thailand) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thailand] . [LINK: ([450]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-457]

[LINK: (Australia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australia]  and   [LINK: (Canada) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canada]  issued a joint statement condemning Iran's use of force against protesters. [LINK: ([445]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-The_New_Region-452]

[LINK: (China) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China]  – On 23 January 2026, China voted against a  [LINK: (UN Human Rights Council) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Nations_Human_Rights_Council]  resolution that condemned Iran's crackdown on nationwide protests. [LINK: ([451]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-aljazeera-2026-1-24-458]

[LINK: (France) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/France] ,   [LINK: (Germany) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Germany]  and the   [LINK: (United Kingdom) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Kingdom] 's  [LINK: (Emmanuel Macron) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emmanuel_Macron] ,  [LINK: (Friedrich Merz) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Friedrich_Merz]  and  [LINK: (Keir Starmer) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Keir_Starmer] , respectively,  released a joint statement on 9 January urging Iran to exercise restraint. [LINK: ([69]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Christou-2026b-72] 
Chancellor Friedrich Merz separately spoke about the violence against Iranian protesters: "This violence is not an expression of strength, but rather a sign of weakness. This violence must end", [LINK: ([452]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-459]  later saying "If a regime can only keep itself in power by force, then it's effectively at the end. I believe we are now seeing the final days and weeks of this regime. In any case, it has no legitimacy through elections in the population. The population is now rising up against this regime." [LINK: ([453]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-460]  [LINK: ([454]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-461]

Chancellor Friedrich Merz separately spoke about the violence against Iranian protesters: "This violence is not an expression of strength, but rather a sign of weakness. This violence must end", [LINK: ([452]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-459]  later saying "If a regime can only keep itself in power by force, then it's effectively at the end. I believe we are now seeing the final days and weeks of this regime. In any case, it has no legitimacy through elections in the population. The population is now rising up against this regime." [LINK: ([453]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-460]  [LINK: ([454]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-461]

[LINK: (Holy See) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holy_See]  –  [LINK: (Pope Leo XIV) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope_Leo_XIV]  expressed concern. [LINK: ([455]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-462]

[LINK: (India) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/India]  – On 23 January 2026, India voted against a UNHRC resolution condemning the Iranian regime's crackdown on nationwide protests in Iran. [LINK: ([451]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-aljazeera-2026-1-24-458]

[LINK: (Israel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Israel]  – Prime Minister  [LINK: (Benjamin Netanyahu) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benjamin_Netanyahu]  said Israel "[identifies] with the struggle of the Iranian people for freedom, liberty and justice". He also mentioned the possibility that the people would take their fate into their hands. [LINK: ([456]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-463] 
 [LINK: (Mossad) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mossad] , Israel's  [LINK: (intelligence agency) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intelligence_agency] , claimed it was "with [the Iranian protesters] in the field". [LINK: ([457]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Mossad-464]

[LINK: (Mossad) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mossad] , Israel's  [LINK: (intelligence agency) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intelligence_agency] , claimed it was "with [the Iranian protesters] in the field". [LINK: ([457]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Mossad-464]

[LINK: (New Zealand) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Zealand]  – Foreign Minister  [LINK: (Winston Peters) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Winston_Peters]  expressed concern about the killing of protesters and described protests as a "fundamental human right". [LINK: ([458]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-465]

[LINK: (Poland) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poland]  – On 15 January 2026, the  [LINK: (Ministry of Foreign Affairs) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ministry_of_Foreign_Affairs_(Poland)]  summoned the Iranian ambassador to express concern over the growing number of casualties and arrests, and called on Iran to cease violence against the protesters and initiate talks. [LINK: ([459]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-466]

[LINK: (Spain) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spain]  – The  [LINK: (government of Pedro Sánchez) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Third_government_of_Pedro_S%C3%A1nchez]  condemned the violence against demonstrators in Iran. [LINK: ([460]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-467]

[LINK: (United Kingdom) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_Kingdom]  – On 13 January 2026, Foreign Secretary Yvette Cooper summoned the Iranian ambassador over the mass killings. [LINK: ([461]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-468]  British Secretary of State for Transport  [LINK: (Heidi Alexander) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heidi_Alexander]  told  [LINK: (Sky News) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sky_News]  that the current priority is to "stem the violence" in Iran. She said Iran is a threat to the Middle East and represses its own people. [LINK: ([462]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Mason-2026-469] 
 [LINK: (Kemi Badenoch) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kemi_Badenoch] , leader of the opposition  [LINK: (Conservative Party) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conservative_Party_(UK)] , told the  [LINK: (BBC) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BBC]  that she would "not have an issue" with Iranian regime change and said she supported the involvement of the US and its allies. She claimed that Iran posed a direct threat to the UK, saying it would "very happily wipe out the UK if it felt it could get away with it". [LINK: ([462]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Mason-2026-469]

[LINK: (Kemi Badenoch) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kemi_Badenoch] , leader of the opposition  [LINK: (Conservative Party) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conservative_Party_(UK)] , told the  [LINK: (BBC) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BBC]  that she would "not have an issue" with Iranian regime change and said she supported the involvement of the US and its allies. She claimed that Iran posed a direct threat to the UK, saying it would "very happily wipe out the UK if it felt it could get away with it". [LINK: ([462]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Mason-2026-469]

[LINK: (United States) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States]  – US president  [LINK: (Donald Trump) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Trump]  warned that the United States would intervene if Iranian authorities violently suppressed "peaceful protests", [LINK: ([463]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-470]  and stated that the US was "locked and loaded and ready to go". [LINK: ([464]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-471]  Trump later said that US is considering "very strong options" as a response to the Iran protests, among them possible military intervention, and he said: "we will hit them at levels that they've never been hit before". [LINK: ([465]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-472]  On 15 January, multiple sources reported that  [LINK: (Carrier Strike Group 3) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carrier_Strike_Group_3]  was expected to arrive in the Middle East in "about a week", and The New York Times reported that an array of combat and refuelling aircraft "were expected to start flowing into the region soon". [LINK: ([466]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-473]  [LINK: ([467]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-474]  On 16 January, Trump thanked the Iranian government for promising to stop the 800 planned executions. [LINK: ([407]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-trump-thank-1-414]  [LINK: ([408]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-trump-thank-2-415]   [LINK: (Steve Witkoff) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steve_Witkoff] , the U.S. Special Envoy to the Middle East, reportedly held a secret meeting with  [LINK: (Reza Pahlavi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reza_Pahlavi] , the exiled former Crown Prince of Iran. [LINK: ([468]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-475]  Sources said that Trump is considering attacks to inspire renewed protests. [LINK: ([469]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-476]  Two American officials and two other sources with knowledge told Axios that the Trump administration is hosting senior Israeli and Saudi defense and intelligence officials this week to discuss possible American attacks against Iran. [LINK: ([470]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-477]  On 29 January, an additional warship was sent by the US to Middle East amid Iran tensions. [LINK: ([471]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-478]  On 30 January, The New York Times reported that raids by American soldiers were among potential military options for Iran that Trump was briefed on and he said that he’s given Iran deadline to accept nuclear agreement prior to potential attack. [LINK: ([472]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-479]  [LINK: ([473]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-480]  Saudi defence minister reportedly told America not attacking Iran would "embolden" regime. [LINK: ([474]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-481]

[LINK: (Turkey) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turkey]  – Foreign minister  [LINK: (Hakan Fidan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hakan_Fidan]  stated that the protests in Iran were "being manipulated from abroad by Iran's rivals", including the United States and Israel, expressed a wish for the resolution of the perceived antagonism between Iran and the West through negotiations, and called on Iran to engage in "very genuine reconciliation and cooperation" with other Middle Eastern countries. [LINK: ([475]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-482]

[LINK: (Amnesty International) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amnesty_International]  and  [LINK: (Human Rights Watch) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_Rights_Watch]  condemned the violent repression and documented indiscriminate killings and arrests. [LINK: ([476]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-483]  [LINK: ([477]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-484]

The Mobarizoun Popular Front, a newly-formed  [LINK: (Baloch) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baloch_people]  nationalist organisation, expressed its support for the protests. [LINK: ([23]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Institute_for_the_Study_of_War-2025-23]

The  [LINK: (Army of the Men of the Naqshbandi Order) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Army_of_the_Men_of_the_Naqshbandi_Order]  expressed support for the Iranian opposition against the Iranian government in early January, calling the Iranian government a "fraudulent  [LINK: (mullah) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mullah]  regime". [LINK: ([478]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-485]

The  [LINK: (Kurdistan Free Life Party) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kurdistan_Free_Life_Party]  (PJAK) co-chair Amir Kerimi stated that the Islamic Republic had lost its social  [LINK: (legitimacy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_legitimacy]  and described the situation as an opportunity to build "a democratic and  [LINK: (decentralised) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Decentralised]  Iran ... that transcends the  [LINK: (nation-state) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nation-state] , based on the  [LINK: (self-governance) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-governance]  of the people", while indicating "the construction of a new authoritarian regime through Reza Pahlavi" as his biggest concern. [LINK: ([479]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-486] 
Israel's former defence minister,  [LINK: (Yoav Gallant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yoav_Gallant] , stated "At this time, when what matters is the action of the masses on the ground, we need to stay behind and direct things with an invisible hand". [LINK: ([480]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-487]

Israel's former defence minister,  [LINK: (Yoav Gallant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yoav_Gallant] , stated "At this time, when what matters is the action of the masses on the ground, we need to stay behind and direct things with an invisible hand". [LINK: ([480]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-487]

From 9 January 2026, multiple international airlines suspended or cancelled flights to and from Iran due to the protests, the internet blackout, and security concerns.  [LINK: (Turkish Airlines) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turkish_Airlines]  cancelled flights between  [LINK: (Istanbul) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Istanbul]  and Iranian cities including Tehran, Tabriz and Mashhad, while  [LINK: (AJet) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AJet]  and  [LINK: (Pegasus Airlines) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pegasus_Airlines]  suspended all services to Iran. UAE carriers  [LINK: (flydubai) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flydubai]  and  [LINK: (Emirates) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emirates_(airline)]  halted flights to Iranian destinations, and  [LINK: (Qatar Airways) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qatar_Airways]  cancelled several flights from Doha to Iran. In Europe,  [LINK: (Austrian Airlines) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Austrian_Airlines]  cancelled its Vienna-Tehran flight on 9 January.  [LINK: (Lufthansa) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lufthansa] , which had planned to resume flights on 16 January after it suspended them due to regional security concerns, delayed its plans to resume services due to the protests. [LINK: ([481]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-488]  [LINK: ([482]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-489]  [LINK: ([483]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-490]  [LINK: ([193]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Farhat-2026-196]  [LINK: ([484]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-491]

Since the outbreak of the protests, several countries have issued travel advisories or warnings for Iran and have advised their citizens to leave the country. These advisories cite security concerns and potential disruptions to transportation and communications. Countries that have issued such advisories include the United States, [LINK: ([485]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-492]  the United Kingdom, [LINK: ([486]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-493]  Canada, [LINK: ([487]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-494]  Australia, [LINK: ([488]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-495]  Germany, [LINK: ([489]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-496]  France, [LINK: ([490]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-497]  New Zealand, [LINK: ([491]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-498]  Ireland, [LINK: ([492]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-499]  and India. [LINK: ([493]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-500]

A January 2026 Quinnipiac poll of US voters found that 70% oppose US military involvement in Iran, 79% of Democrats, 80% of independents and 53% of Republicans opposed military involvement. [LINK: ([494]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-501]  [LINK: ([495]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-502]  [LINK: ([496]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-503]


# ANALYSIS #

On 30 December, Iran International suggested that the protests were a "historic break" of Iranian bazaar merchants, historically a critical and old ally of the Islamic Republic, from the Iranian government. Such breaks, the news agency suggested, were fuelled by the Islamic Republic's blame towards the merchants as "price gougers" for rejecting state-standardised pricing and being unable to restock market inventories if they complied to their demands. Anger towards the government by merchants were also caused by the proposed  [LINK: (2025–26 Iranian budget) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%9326_Iranian_budget] , which would prioritise  [LINK: (deficit spending) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deficit_spending]  and large tax increases to make up for a decline in oil revenues for government funding. [LINK: ([497]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-504]

On 2 January, Iran International cited the opinion of analysts, including  [LINK: (intelligence analysts) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intelligence_analysis]  and journalists, who suggested that Iran might have entered the early stages of regime collapse. [LINK: ([498]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-505]  On 4 January, according to the  [LINK: (New York Times) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_Times] , Iranian officials, including foreign minister  [LINK: (Abbas Araghchi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abbas_Araghchi] , described the government as being in a "survival mode", with difficulties in either reversing economic problems or handling the military threat of attack by the United States or Israel. Pezeshkian held two emergency meetings following the start of the protests. Some of his advisers recommended that he publicly criticise the role of Khamenei as supreme leader. [LINK: ([284]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-The_New_York_Times-288]  A 5 January analysis in  [LINK: (Foreign Policy) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foreign_Policy]  argues that the 2025–2026 protests differ from the  [LINK: (Mahsa Amini protests) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mahsa_Amini_protests]  in that the 2025–2026 protests are more geographically widespread, including small towns rather than just major cities, and involve a broader range of groups, including students, workers, women, and ethnic minorities. The analysis also saw differences in the international context as significant, with Trump's unpredictability and overt willingness to violate international law as a factor differing from Biden's approach, and the  [LINK: (fall of the Assad regime) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fall_of_the_Assad_regime]  as a weakening of Iran's regional support. The authors also saw the focus of the 2025–2026 protests as shifting from social reform to regime change. [LINK: ([9]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Golkar-2026-9]

In early January 2026,  [LINK: (The Times) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Times]  referred to intelligence reports stating that Khamenei had an escape plan, for him and about twenty close associates and family members, including Khamenei's son  [LINK: (Mojtaba Khamenei) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mojtaba_Khamenei] , ready to flee to Moscow in case security forces defected to the side of the protesters. Beni Sabti, a former Israeli intelligence officer, stated that he expected Moscow to be Khamenei's preferred location of exile if he fled. [LINK: ([292]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Weiniger-2026-298]  Social scientist Mali Rezaei viewed the protests as showing a "deepening rupture between society and the ideological foundations of the state". She argued that one of the factors behind the protests was the context of Iran's  [LINK: (multi-millennial history) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iran#Antiquity]  and  [LINK: (ethnic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ethnicities_in_Iran]  and  [LINK: (cultural) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Culture_of_Iran]  diversity, in which the seventh century CE  [LINK: (Muslim conquest of Persia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muslim_conquest_of_Persia]  left in place "a persistent resistance to absolutism". She pointed to a 2020 GAMAAN study that found that  [LINK: (irreligion in Iran) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irreligion_in_Iran]  and support for  [LINK: (secularism) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Secularism_in_Iran]  was growing. In addition to economic factors, Rezaei saw environmental crises such as the  [LINK: (disappearance of Lake Urmia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lake_Urmia#Falling_level_and_increasing_salinity]  as playing a role. She viewed the Iranian government's destruction of some elements of Persian culture and a "passivity in defending [cultural] legacy" as a weakening of the government's protection of "the nation". Rezaei saw the  [LINK: (Mahsa Amini protests) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mahsa_Amini_protests]  and the  [LINK: (Woman, Life, Freedom) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woman,_Life,_Freedom]  slogan as a key turning point in which the protest movement evolved to avoid cooptation by either the government and individual celebrities. She viewed the pro-Pahlavi slogans as mainly representing a desire for a secular democracy, not absolute monarchy, that would recover national  [LINK: (agency) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agency_(sociology)] . [LINK: ([499]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-506]

On 26 January,  [LINK: (The New York Times) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_New_York_Times]  cited US intelligence reports viewing the Iranian government as being in its politically weakest position since the 1979  [LINK: (Iranian Revolution) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iranian_Revolution] . The NYT stated that a US military buildup near Iran was ongoing and viewed it as unclear if the US administration had taken a decision for a military attack on Iran. [LINK: ([500]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-507]

On 10 January, human rights activist Hamid Enayat suggested that "dozens of instances" of disarmament of security forces during the protests, and the "breakdown of the deterrent function" of the Iranian state's  [LINK: (monopoly of violence) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monopoly_of_violence]  indicated a  [LINK: (transition to a new phase) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complex_system_approach_to_peace_and_armed_conflict#Phase_transitions] , that of an  [LINK: (uprising) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uprising] . Enayat cited cases of protesters stopping security forces on buses, disarming them and tying their hands, and a case of a stun gun being taken from a security forces member and used against him. He argued that  [LINK: (Malekshahi County) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malekshahi_County]  effectively came under insurgent control on 6 January when security forces fled from the protesters. He saw the protests as having shifted to a phase in which the Iranian state had lost its power to frighten citizens into obedience. [LINK: ([501]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Hamid_Enayat-2026-508]

On 11 January, historian  [LINK: (Mark Almond) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mark_Almond]  disagreed with comparison of the Iranian protests to the  [LINK: (fall of the Berlin Wall) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fall_of_the_Berlin_Wall]  in November 1989. He argued that it would be more like the  [LINK: (Storming of the Bastille) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Storming_of_the_Bastille] , because, if successful, the overthrow of the Islamic Republic would be a rebellion against an internally developed regime, rather than one imposed internationally. He argued that the protests already qualified for the term "revolution". [LINK: ([502]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-509]  In contradistinction most Israeli analysts believed that the conditions for successful revolution had not yet been met, the existential threat to the regime notwithstanding. [LINK: ([503]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-510]  [LINK: ([504]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-511]  A minority view, such as held by retired  [LINK: (Brigadier General) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brigadier_General]   [LINK: (Amir Avivi) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amir_Avivi] , chairman of the Israel Defence and Security Forum (IDSF), is that the Iranian regime faces imminent collapse. [LINK: ([505]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-512]

On 13 January 2026, German Chancellor  [LINK: (Friedrich Merz) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Friedrich_Merz]  stated that "we are now witnessing the final days and weeks of this regime". He argued, "If a regime can only stay in power through violence, then it is effectively finished." [LINK: ([506]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:82-513]  Historian Ali Ansari argued that while the Iranian regime has long relied on violence to maintain control, it now faces a fragile state with dwindling resources and growing public disenchantment. Ansari notes, "The Islamic Republic has few options that don't include its own demise", pointing to the rising anger and fear among the population, with many now believing the regime has no future. This sentiment marks a turning point, as even the previously apathetic are now rejecting the system. [LINK: ([507]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-514]  According to  [LINK: (The Economist) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Economist] , Iran's leadership, having resorted to violence to maintain power, may be at a breaking point. According to their analysis, "Iran's rulers are merciless because of their weakness. They have nowhere to turn and nothing to offer their people but violence." It concludes that this could be "the moment that ends the theocrats' 47 years in power". [LINK: ([508]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-515]

An analysis in  [LINK: (Prospect) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prospect_(magazine)]  examined four possible scenarios of change if the government fell. [LINK: ([509]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:17-516]  [LINK: ([391]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-330000injured-398]   [LINK: (HRANA) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_Rights_Activists_in_Iran] 's total of confirmed deaths and deaths under investigation reached 22,490 on 24 January. [LINK: ([399]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-HRANA_28thday-406]  According to one scenario, the removal of Khamenei from power would create a power vacuum, while senior bureaucrats would continue to run the state. Competition between the remaining elites would bring about the emergence of a temporary government, as a result of compromise of all sides. [LINK: ([509]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:17-516]  A second scenario suggests that the security establishment would sacrifice clerical rule, including Khamenei, for the sake of seizing power. Coercive measures would be used to prevent disorder and the new leadership "would present itself as the guardian of stability, national unity and territorial integrity," while replacing revolutionary ideology with pragmatism. [LINK: ([509]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:17-516]  The third scenario is of simultaneous collapse of both political authority and repressive forces, causing different branches of the IRGC and political factions to compete for control, possibly sparking civil war, with the potential of intervention by foreign forces. [LINK: ([509]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:17-516]  According to Prospect, the final, and most preferred, scenario is the return of Reza Pahlavi, who would assume power while the existing government collapses, some senior clerics flee to Iraq, some security forces flee to Russia, and most of the IRGC is absorbed into the armed forces. [LINK: ([509]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-:17-516]

Other outlets, including  [LINK: (CNN) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CNN]  and the  [LINK: (Institute for the Study of War) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Institute_for_the_Study_of_War] , suggested that groups such as the Kurdistan Freedom Party and the Kurdistan National Guard attacked government military bases and caused severe casualties. [LINK: ([223]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-KFParty-226]  [LINK: ([20]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025%E2%80%932026_Iranian_protests#cite_note-Sleiman-2026-20]


